Abstract
\par
Adaptive management involves learning-oriented decision making in the presence of uncer-
\par
tainty about the responses of a resource system to management. It is implemented through
\par
an iterative sequence of decision making, monitoring and assessment of system responses,
\par
and incorporating what is learned into future decision making. Decision making at each
\par
point is informed by a value or objective function, for example total harvest anticipated over
\par
some time frame. The value function expresses the value associated with decisions, and it
\par
is influenced by system status as updated through monitoring. Often, decision making fol-
\par
lows shortly after a monitoring event. However, it is certainly possible for the cadence of
\par
decision making to differ from that of monitoring. In this paper we consider different combi-
\par
nations of annual and biennial decision making, along with annual and biennial monitoring.
\par
With biennial decision making decisions are changed only every other year; with biennial
\par
monitoring field data are collected only every other year. Different cadences of decision
\par
making combine with annual and biennial monitoring to define 4 scenarios. Under each sce-
\par
nario we describe optimal valuations for active and passive adaptive decision making. We
\par
highlight patterns in valuation among scenarios, depending on the occurrence of monitoring
\par
and decision making events. Differences between years are tied to the fact that every other
\par
year a new decision can be made no matter what the scenario, and state information is avail-
\par
able to inform that decision. In the subsequent year, however, in 3 of the 4 scenarios either a
\par
decision is repeated or monitoring does not occur (or both). There are substantive differ-
\par
ences in optimal values among the scenarios, as well as the optimal policies producing
\par
those values. Especially noteworthy is the influence of monitoring cadence on valuation in
\par
some years. We highlight patterns in policy and valuation among the scenarios, and discuss
\par
management implications and extensions.
\par
\textbf{Introduction}
\par
A well-known approach to learning-oriented decision making in natural resources is adaptive
\par
management, in which learning occurs through recursive management and what is learned at
\par
each time is used to guide future management actions (Williams and Brown [1--2]). Adaptive
\par
PLOS ONE \textbar{} https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0182934 August 11, 2017 1 / 18Decision frequencies in adaptive management
\par
decision making is based on the recognition that resource systems are only partially under-
\par
stood, and there is value in tracking resource conditions and applying what is learned as the
\par
resources are being managed (Williams [3]). In the ongoing process of learning and adapta-
\par
tion, adjustments to decision making occur as understanding improves, with the ultimate goal
\par
of improved management (Walters [4]).
\par
Adaptive decision making is by its nature flexible, and therefore is applicable to a wide vari-
\par
ety of resource problems (Williams and Brown [5]). In some instances its focus is on the
\par
improvement of understanding about the role of management in influencing resource dynam-
\par
ics (Linkov et al. [6], Runge et al. [7]). In others it is on the social and institutional framework
\par
supporting iterative decision making (Susskind et al. [8], Convertino et al. [9]). In yet others, it
\par
is on the ``architecture'' of structured decision making, with the elicitation of values, objectives,
\par
decision alternatives etc. (Johnson et al. [10], Linkov et al. [11]). Even if one is primarily con-
\par
cerned about uncertainty and the improvement of technical understanding, the range of appli-
\par
cability is extremely broad. An important challenge for an adaptive framework is to cover a
\par
large number of decision problems, yet be flexible enough that it can be tailored to the details
\par
of any particular problem.
\par
Here we take a formal, decision-theoretic approach to adaptive management (Johnson and
\par
Williams [12]), rather than more ad hoc approaches that sometimes are described in the litera-
\par
ture (e.g., Schhreiber et al. [13]). In particular, we focus on an iterative sequencing of (i) deci-
\par
sion making and taking actions, (ii) followed by monitoring of system responses, (iii) followed
\par
by assessment of data, (iv) followed by incorporating what is learned into future decision mak-
\par
ing (Fig 1). System state is typically monitored at fixed intervals, often annually, in order to
\par
inform decisions that occur with the same frequency (Hauser et al. [14]).
\par
Alternatives to the coincidence of decision making and monitoring involve different
\par
cadences for the 2 activities. For example, the setting of migratory bird hunting regulations
\par
often involves annual monitoring and decision-making, but a different sequence was adopted
\par
for pink-footed geese (Anserbrachyrhynchus) in Europe (Johnson and Madsen [15]). In the
\par
latter case, administrative burden is reduced by fixing harvest quotas for three years, while
\par
population monitoring occurs annually. Thus, while learning accrues annually, decisions are
\par
based on system state only every fourth year. In the United States, the regulations setting pro-
\par
cess for duck harvest has recently been modified so that current system state is not known
\par
(monitored) at the time a decision must be made (Johnson et al. [16]). Decisions must there-
\par
fore be conditioned on the previous system state and regulatory action.
\par
The issue of timing in decisions and monitoring has arisen in other decision processes as
\par
well. An example is the adaptive management program adopted by the Atlantic States Marine
\par
Fishery Commission for the establishment of horseshoe crab harvest in Delaware Bay quotas
\par
(Smith et al. [17]). State variables relevant to harvest decisions include not only the abundance
\par
of the harvested species, but also the abundance of migratory shorebirds (red knots [Calidris
\par
canutus]) that depend on horseshoe crab eggs as a food source at key migration stopover sites
\par
in Delaware Bay. Harvest quotas for the fishing season of June-December, year t+1 are estab-
\par
lished in the fall (e.g., November) of year t. The decisions are informed by estimates of system
\par
state variables obtained in May of year t(red knots) and October-November of year t-1 (horse-
\par
shoe crabs [Limuluspolyphemus]). Debate has ensued about the feasibility of pushing the har-
\par
vest decision forward (e.g., January, year t+1) in order to make use of the previous fall's crab
\par
survey data.
\par
More generally, disconnecting the sequencing of decision making and monitoring is poten-
\par
tially advantageous, in that cost-savings often can be obtained by reducing the frequency of
\par
monitoring, or alternatively reducing the frequency of analysis and decision making. Of
\par
course, an important question concerns the effect of such asynchrony, in particular as it relates
\par
