\par
to the value produced by decisions made when there is restricted decision making or an
\par
absence of monitoring information. The issue can be framed in terms of the value produced
\par
with management policy that is informed by monitoring. One approach is to identify a value
\par
function, for example the expected accumulated harvest of a biological population, which
\par
expresses the value associated with decision making given the status of the resource being
\par
managed and some measure of understanding of it. If the resource is managed optimally based
\par
on current information about it, the question at issue is whether and to what degree the value
\par
produced through decision making is compromised by an asynchrony between decision mak-
\par
ing and monitoring.
\par
Our objective here is to provide a framework by which to consider the question of asyn-
\par
chrony in monitoring and decision making, by describing and assessing valuation forms for 4
\par
simple and plausible scenarios. These involve annual and biennial decision making in combi-
\par
nation with annual and biennial monitoring. With biennial decision making, decisions are
\par
changed only every other year; with biennial monitoring, field data are collected only every
