\par
agement factors into future decision making only after the current decision is made. The
\par
absence of anticipated learning in guiding decisions is indicated by the use of current model
\par
state qt in the value term V[xt+1,qt] in Eq (6). We note that our description of passive adaptive
\par
management extends beyond many descriptions in the literature, where passive adaptive man-
\par
agement is held to involve actions based on the best available model, followed by post-decision
\par
monitoring to revise or replace the model (Walters and Hilborn [25], Schreiber et al. [13], Wil-
\par
liams [24]).
\par
While the value V[xt,qt] produced by passive adaptive management is necessarily less than
\par
that of active adaptive management, the passive form has the advantage of being computation-
\par
ally tractable for relatively large problems, specifically because only the current model state
\par
must be considered. In practice, policies and values may vary little between the active and pas-
\par
sive forms (Johnson et al. [26], Hauser and Possingham [27]).
\par
\textbf{Valuation under different cadences of decision making and}
\par
\textbf{monitoring}
\par
The learning-based approach described above involves iterative decision making through
\par
time, utilizing monitoring information that is collected at each decision point. However, the
\par
selection of decisions need not coincide with the monitoring of system transitions. In what fol-
\par
lows we consider annual and biennial decision making along with annual and biennial moni-
\par
toring, where biennial decision making involves changes in decisions only every other year
\par
and biennial monitoring means the collection of field data every other year. The options for
\par
decision making combine with those for monitoring to define 4 scenarios. Here we discuss
\par
optimal valuations for each scenario, and compare/contrast the valuations among scenarios.
\par
We acknowledge that variations in timing beyond the biennial cadences considered here are
\par
possible, and we highlight other examples in the discussion below.
\par
Given the scenarios defined by annual and biennial cadences, every 2 years a new decision
\par
can be made and state information is available to inform that decision. In the subsequent year
\par
after a new decision, however, in 3 of the 4 scenarios either the decision is repeated or moni-
\par
toring does not occur (or both). The difference among scenarios becomes clear by focusing on
\par
the arguments of the value function V(At \textbar{} xt,qt).
\par
{$\bullet$} Scenario 1: Annual decision making and annual monitoring
\par
{$\bullet$} Every year (xt,qt) is known because of annual monitoring
\par
{$\bullet$} A new action can be taken every year
\par
{$\bullet$} Scenario 2: Annual decision making and biennial monitoring
\par
{$\bullet$} Every other year (xt,qt) is not known because of the lack of monitoring
\par
{$\bullet$} A new action can be taken every year
\par
