\par
{$\bullet$} Scenario 3: Biennial decision making and annual monitoring:
\par
{$\bullet$} Every year (xt,qt) is known because of annual monitoring
\par
{$\bullet$} The same action is taken in successive years
\par
{$\bullet$} Scenario 4: Biennial decision making and biennial monitoring
\par
{$\bullet$} Every other year (xt,qt) is not known because of the lack of monitoring
\par
{$\bullet$} The same action is taken in successive years
\par
The differences among scenarios are accentuated in non-monitoring years. In this situation
\par
scenarios 1 and 2 produce different valuations, because the state is seen via monitoring under
\par
scenario 1 but not under scenario 2. Scenarios 3 and 4 also produce different valuations, for
\par
the same reason: the state is seen via monitoring under scenario 3 but not under scenario 4.
\par
Finally, the valuations for the scenarios 1 and 2 differ from those for scenarios 3 and 4, because
\par
actions in successive years are repeated in scenarios 3 and 4.
\par
In the next sections we assume active adaptive decision making in the development of valu-
\par
ation forms. We then describe valuation under passive adaptive management. In both cases we
\par
use V(At \textbar{} xt,qt) as in Eq (3) to represent the aggregate value associated with policy At given the
\par
combination (xt,qt) of system and model states, and use V[xt,qt] as in Eqs (5) and (7) to repre-
\par
sent the optimal valuation obtained by maximizing V(At \textbar{} xt,qt) over all available policies.
\par
