\textbf{Patterns in the optimal valuations}
\par
Several informative comparisons can be recognized among the scenarios, in terms of the
\par
actions to be optimized and the state information that is available when actions are to be
\par
selected.
\par
Comparisons of annual and biennial monitoring
\par
For the scenarios considered here, in a year twhen monitoring is conducted and new decisions
\par
are made the valuation of optimal policy is the same irrespective of monitoring frequency.
\par
That is, the same expression for optimal valuation obtains under both monitoring regimes.
\par
For annual decision making the optimal valuation for annual decision making is V[xt,qt] irre-
\par
spective of the cadence of monitoring. For biennial decision making the optimal valuation is
\par
V0[xt,qt]. Basically, if one knows the system and model states when decisions are made, there is
\par
no additional value in collecting more information between decision points (but see below on
\par
the potential influence of partial observability). This pattern holds for annual as well as bien-
\par
nial decision making.
