\textbf{Discussion}
\par
As adaptive management continues to grow in its popularity and use in natural resources,
\par
there is a trend toward being more flexible in its implementation. But greater flexibility in turn
\par
creates new challenges in capturing an appropriate decision making ``architecture'' for individ-
\par
ual problems. An important example concerns the frequencies of decision making and moni-
\par
toring. In particular, an accounting is needed of the effects of different cadences on both
\par
strategy and valuation. Here we have described a technical framework that allows for assess-
\par
ment of differing combinations of annual and biennial decision making and monitoring.
\par
In this paper we have highlighted substantive differences in value functions for varying
\par
cadences of decision making and monitoring, recognizing that the differences are less pro-
\par
nounced for years when a system is observed. Indeed, for a year twith observed status the
\par
cadence of monitoring does not affect valuation (or policy) for either annual or biennial deci-
\par
sion making. On the other hand, the cadence of monitoring does affect valuation and policy
\par
for year t+1 in which the system is not observed.
\par
These patterns provide insight into the value of the information that is added with more fre-
\par
quent monitoring (Yokota and Thompson [30], Williams and Johnson [28]). For example, in
\par
a monitoring year the valuations under less frequent and more frequent monitoring are identi-
\par
cal, so no value is added by increasing the frequency of monitoring. But there is an added
\par
value in the subsequent year, as a result of the need to average the valuations across system
\par
states with less frequent monitoring. The gain in value with more frequent monitoring is the
\par
difference between a valuation informed by knowledge of system state (annual monitoring),
\par
versus an average valuation when system state is only known stochastically (biennial monitor-
\par
ing). The results of such an assessment can be useful to managers as a metric in determining
\par
whether to reduce annual to biennial monitoring, or to expand biennial to annual monitoring.
\par
The assessment for annual and biennial cadences can be extended to include options for 3
\par
or more years. Thus, one could consider the effect of decision making every 3 years rather
\par
than every year, or the effect of monitoring every 3 years rather than every year (Johnson and
\par
Madsen [15]). One way to assess such an extension would be to express returns in the value
\par
functions in terms of 3 time steps rather than 2, and compute the corresponding expectations.
\par
The practical effect would be to complicate the mathematical expressions for valuation, and
\par
likely would make more difficult the comparative interpretation of patterns.
\par
Other variations in the cadence of monitoring and decision making are possible. In the
\par
above, biennial monitoring and decision making occur in the same years, which allows deci-
\par
sions to be informed by system and model states at those times. Another variation is for moni-
\par
toring and decision making to occur in alternative years, for example with decision making in
\par
one year and monitoring to occur in the subsequent year. The overall effect of this cadence is
\par
to require averaging based on prior year status each time a new decision is made. Yet another
\par
PLOS ONE \textbar{} https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0182934 August 11, 2017 14 / 18Decision frequencies in adaptive management
\par
variation involves decision making prior to monitoring each year, so that the monitoring
\par
results are not available to inform the selection of actions for that year (Johnson et al. [16]).
\par
Under these conditions, possible actions must again be conditioned on the previous system
\par
and model state and the action previously taken.
\par
The lack of additional value in collecting information between decision points that is
\par
highlighted here depends on the assumption that the resource system is fully observable. An
\par
allowance for partial observability defines a partially observable Markov decision process
\par
(POMDP), in which system status is approximated by a time-specific probability distribution
\par
or ``belief state'' that is updated with monitoring data through time (Kaelbling et al. [31], Litt-
\par
man [32]). A natural accounting of both partial observability and structural uncertainty con-
\par
siders the updating of belief, whenever it occurs, as a factor in the updating of model state, so
\par
that a change is belief affects the propagation of model state and in so doing may influence pol-
\par
icy and valuation. Under these circumstances monitoring between decision times can have an
\par
effect on decision making.
\par
There is a very large technical literature on theory and applications of adaptive management
\par
in natural resources for fully observable systems, and a much smaller but growing literature of
\par
POMDP methods and applications in natural resources (e.g., Lane, D. [33], Chade \textasciiacute{}
\par
s et al. [34],
\par
Haight and Polasky [35], Tomberlin [36], Chade \textasciiacute{}
\par
s et al. [37], Fackler and Haight [38], Regan
\par
et al. [39], Nicol and Chade \textasciiacute{}
\par
s [40]). However, there are very few expositions concerning natural
\par
resources that include both (Williams [20], Fackler and Pacifici [41], Chade \textasciiacute{}
\par
s et al. [42]), even
\par
though structural uncertainty and partial observability are common in natural resources. The
\par
limited documentation is no doubt a result, at least partially, of the formidable difficulties of
\par
incorporating both factors in analytic and computational frameworks that are accessible to
\par
natural resources specialists (e.g., Jaulmes et al. [43], Williams [20], Bertsekas [29]). One rather
\par
ad hoc approach is to assume full observability, identify optimal policies and valuations as
\par
approximations to the broader problem that includes partial observability, and explore the sen-
\par
sitivity of the approximations to errors in state estimation.
\par
Finally, a key determinant in the usefulness of comparative valuation with different
\par
cadences is the ability to actually compute values with the forms of the value functions dis-
\par
cussed above. Software is currently available for active and passive adaptive management
\par
under annual decision making and monitoring (Lubow [44], Fackler [45]). It is straightfor-
\par
ward to use this software for the case of biennial decision making and monitoring for passive
\par
adaptive management, by utilizing the 2-step transition probabilities. Further software devel-
\par
opment is necessary for the other scenarios, which involves a greater or lesser degree of diffi-
\par
culty and programming effort depending on the scenario.
\par
