\par
Abstract Local governments have implemented official websites, making
\par
e-Government a holistic access platform for modern public administration service
\par
provision. However, academic and managerial knowledge about the success of
\par
e-Government remains limited. Given that citizen needs have become a focus of
\par
interest, it is reasonable to investigate its success factors from a user-oriented perspec-
\par
tive. Existing scientific knowledge does not comprehensively explain the usage inten-
\par
tions of today's heterogeneous e-Government city portal users. Hence, this study
\par
conceptualizes important determinants of local e-Government portals identifying key
\par
factors that determine intention to use from a citizen viewpoint.
