\par
Introduction
\par
In the past decades, administrative bodies have incrementally established e-Government
\par
city portals on the local level in order to empower and improve public services, especially
\par
for citizens (Im et al. 2012). TheseBmulti-functional information systems provide a single
\par
point of access to relevant information and services via the Web-enabled interface\textasciicircum{}
\par
(eDevelopment 2012). They also provide a focal point of contact for all citizens (Welch
\par
et al. 2005) and fundamentally influence citizen-government-interaction (Charalabidis
\par
et al. 2006). Although these portals are supposed to bring along manifold potentials, such
\par
* Bernd W. Wirtz
\par
ls-wirtz@uni-speyer.de
\par
Oliver Tuna Kurtz
\par
kurtz@uni-speyer.de
\par
1 Chair for Information and Communication Management, German University of Administrative
\par
Sciences Speyer, Freiherr-vom-Stein-Str. 2, 67346 Speyer, Germany354 B.W. Wirtz, O.T. Kurtz
\par
as reduced costs and enhanced service quality (Karunasena and Deng 2012), public
\par
authorities face major implementation challenges. Accordingly, the full potential of
\par
e-Government has not been exploited yet in public administration practice (Capgemini
\par
et al. 2013). In this context, public management officials of local e-Governments have to
\par
meet the high expectations (Weerakkody et al. 2013) arising from the implementation of
\par
new technologies (United Nations 2014; Arellano-Gault 2012) and from a backlog of
\par
efficiently exploiting e-Government potentials (Arduini et al. 2011).
\par
In fact, some countries show significant advances in providing public local e-Services
\par
and some local e-Governments claim to be already user-oriented and interactive (Wirtz
\par
et al. 2015; Jun and Weare 2011). However, local e-Government overall is still stuck in
\par
delivering information online and just providing a few transactions and limited interac-
\par
tivity (Norris and Reddick 2013). Considering its current state, Norris and Reddick
\par
(2013) emphasize,BLocal e-Government is also mainly one way, from government to
\par
citizens, and there is little or no evidence that it is transformative in any way.\textasciicircum{} The
\par
reason for that is seen in an unduly focus on the information systems (IS) technology
\par
perspective of portal management, as even the most developed e-Government websites
\par
are shortened by a deficit of citizen-centric services (Tan et al. 2013). Hence, e-
\par
Government officers need to redesign processes while concentrating on user-oriented
\par
service provision and citizen needs. However, this has become a complex challenge
\par
since citizens' requirements vary and change over time (Venkatesh et al. 2012). For this
\par
reason, a successful e-Government project continuously calls for new e-Government
\par
strategies of portal management. Thus, public administrations can fulfill citizens' needs
\par
and identify success factors of e-Government (Wirtz and Piehler 2016). To support this
\par
achievement, the scientific discourse on e-Government success should provide policy
\par
recommendations for portal management regarding a user-friendly city portal that is
\par
adapted to citizen needs. However, a review of existing e-Government research reveals
\par
that most research contributions are provider-perspective studies (Verdegem and Verleye
\par
2009). Generally, factors determining e-Government success have not been analyzed
\par
sufficiently (Wirtz and Daiser 2016; Arduini et al. 2011). Morgeson et al. (2011) support
\par
this view and claim that additional empirical findings are necessary to better understand
\par
the behavior of e-Government users.
\par
Research Objectives and Proceedings of the Study
\par
The existing research contributions frequently involve overly broad and generic models
\par
that are not conceptually expanded (Alawneh et al. 2013). Several studies attempt to
\par
explain citizens' e-Government intentions and usage behavior by using attitudinal
\par
multivariate approaches. These studies addressing success factors of e-Government
\par
usage are primarily based on established models of information system research
\par
(B\'elanger and Carter 2012; Rana et al. 2011). Here, Verdegem and Verleye (2009)
\par
emphasize that e-Government studies mostly take a supply perspective in conducting
\par
the models with respect to technological foundations rather than considering user needs
\par
of e-Government. However, scientists cannot fully evaluate usage behavior of citizens
\par
by applying technology-oriented information system models. Thus, they need to shift
\par
the methodological scientific e-Government discourse to a more user and service-
\par
oriented perspective since getting insights into citizens' preferences may fundamentally
\par
influence e-Government success (Wirtz et al. 2016; Venkatesh et al. 2012).Determinants of Citizen Usage Intentions in e-Government 355
\par
Apart from that, the focus has shifted towards innovative and up-to-date e-Services
\par
(Kaisara and Pather 2011) due to the increased importance of transactions between
\par
citizens and administration, the partly participatory character of modern public e-
\par
Services (Royo et al. 2014), and the expansion of e-Government channels, e.g. in
\par
terms of mobile applications (Hung et al. 2013). However, prior scientific discussions
\par
on e-Government success generally do not consider specific up-to-date and innovative
\par
e-Government services. Consequently, this study focuses on the demand of citizens by
\par
conducting a user-oriented investigation identifying specific and innovative key e-
\par
Government services.
\par
Prior user-centric research shows that beside user satisfaction, the endogenous
\par
construct of intention to use is a widely used measure of success (Davis et al. 1989;
\par
Swanson 1974) that scholars increasingly adapt to the use of e-Government systems.
\par
When measuring intention to use e-Government city portals, it is indispensable to
\par
identify the users' needs by observing specific public e-Services. Only in this way e-
\par
Governments may address citizens' concerns effectively and foster the usage of these
\par
services as it often weakens for a lack of initial prognoses (Detlor et al. 2013). Against
\par
this background, Almahamid (2013) argues that e-Government is far from reaching its
\par
maximum potential, as the gap between offered and used e-Government services is still
\par
large. Moreover, e-Government city portals have the most authority over governmental
\par
procedures and decisions (Lim and Tang 2006). They are therefore an integral part of
\par
modern public service provision. Hence, examining e-Government at the local level
\par
represents an ideal basis for transferring findings to higher administrative entities (Liu
\par
et al. 2010). Based on these observations and the fact that scientific examinations
\par
concerning e-Government city portals remain underinvestigated (Wirtz et al. 2014;
\par
Morgeson et al. 2011), this study analyzes local e-Government city portals.
\par
Facing the issues of portal management of modern public administrations and
\par
corresponding deficits in current e-Government research, this study aims to conceptu-
\par
alize a user-oriented model for measuring the intention to use e-Government city portals.
\par
Hence, this study identifies and examines factors related to specific public e-Services of
\par
e-Government city portals. In this context, the investigation applies constructs that are
\par
consistent with the needs of users in modern public administration practice. Therefore,
\par
the study seeks to answer the following core research question: Which public e-Services
\par
determine the intention to use e-Government city portals from a citizen perspective? We
\par
address this question in the following steps. The upcoming section provides the basis by
\par
presenting a review of related e-Government research. Based on this literature review,
\par
the subsequent section develops the proposed hypotheses of the research model. The
\par
core of the study empirically tests the developed hypotheses by using binary logistic
\par
regression analysis. The final section presents the findings and discusses implications
\par
for research and practitioners of e-Government city portal management.
\par
Literature Review of e-Government Usage Intention
\par
Although e-Government is shifting towards innovative and user-oriented public
\par
e-Services (Kaisara and Pather 2011), the current scientific discourse often neglects
\par
this shift and rather focuses on e-Commerce and technology than on users. In particular,
\par
existing studies frequently adapt established theoretical and technology-oriented or356 B.W. Wirtz, O.T. Kurtz
\par
marketing-related approaches to the public administration context. Thereby, they use
\par
abstract constructs that are generic and aggregated (Ozkan and Kanat 2011; Rana et al.
\par
2011). With regard to studies that integrate the Technology Acceptance Model (TAM)
\par
or related technology acceptance-based models, the most frequently used constructs are
\par
usefulness and ease of use. Public administration research likewise demonstrates the
\par
traditional distinct efficiency of these two main variables of the TAM in the e-
\par
Government context (Rana et al. 2012). Findings suggest that perceived ease of use
\par
directly affects perceived usefulness, which in turn affects intention to use e-Govern-
\par
ment. In the context of studies adapting the Delone and McLean IS Success Model
\par
(D\&M) model, information, system and service quality influence intention to use e-
\par
Government. However, in contrast to traditional IS research, the analysis of system
\par
quality indicates that the relationship of the construct with intention to use is less
\par
strong. Studies applying the Diffusion of Innovation Theory (DOI) predict that the
\par
independent constructs compatibility, relative advantage, complexity and image all
\par
have a direct relationship with the dependent construct intention to use. Finally,
\par
considering the multi-theoretic studies combining two or more theories of those listed
\par
above, scientists most frequently analyze the constructsBlack of trust in the
\par
government\textasciicircum{} andBlack of trust in the internet\textasciicircum. The results indicate that trust has a
\par
significant effect in explaining the adoption of e-Government.
\par
Although e-Government usage intention research is in an early stage, it offers some
\par
important insights for the public sector. However, despite the fundamental transforma-
\par
tion of public administrations in the light of rapid ICT development and changed online
\par
user preferences, prior research lacks a distinct understanding of the factors that
\par
determine intention to use e-Government. One reason for this is the partly inappropriate
\par
suitability of technology-oriented IS and e-Commerce models to the e-Government
\par
domain, since the characteristics in the e-Government context differ considerably from
\par
those in the IS and e-Commerce context (Al-Haddad et al. 2011). The mere appliance
\par
of IS and e-Commerce models in the e-Government context is an approach that does
\par
not adequately consider system, service and user specific differences of e-Government
\par
portals. Furthermore, frequently used traditional constructs such as usefulness and ease
\par
of use are partly inappropriate drivers to measure intention to use e-Government
\par
portals. The same applies to meta-emotional constructs that focus on the emotional
\par
status of an individual user, such as trust in the internet, perceived credibility, social
\par
influence, risk perception, subjective norm, self-efficacy etc. These constructs impede
\par
the identification of appropriate management parameters for an efficient user-oriented
\par
e-Government portal management.
\par
Given these observations, this study identifies constructs that are detached from
\par
traditional technology-acceptance perspectives. These constructs explicitly consider
\par
specific innovative public e-Services on the local level reflecting the needs of citizens
\par
and meeting the technological requirements and possibilities of modern e-Government
\par
city portals.
\par
Hypotheses Development and Research Model
\par
For the conceptualization of the research model and the investigation of appropriate
\par
determinants of the intention to use e-Government city portals, the study combinesDeterminants of Citizen Usage Intentions in e-Government 357
\par
qualitative and quantitative analysis within a mixed-method approach (Teddlie and
\par
Tashakkori 2006). Scholars regard a mixed-method design as more effective than a
\par
mono-method approach, since it usually provides comprehensive insights and thus a
\par
broader perspective (Davis et al. 2011; Molina Azor\'in and Cameron 2010). In the
\par
context of the present study design, the authors have carried out a three-stage analysis.
\par
First, they have extensively reviewed the scientific literature on the intention to use e-
\par
Government. In the second stage, they have performed an exploratory website analysis
\par
of five international e-Government city portals. In terms of a gap analysis, the authors
\par
have analyzed the best practice city portals of Hong Kong, London, and New York
\par
(Waseda University 2013), as well as two further German city portals - Berlin and
\par
Mainz.1 In the third stage, they have presented the results to six public administration
\par
experts to validate the deductions of the first and second stage.
\par
Regarding the first stage, the authors have examined all peer-reviewed empirical
\par
publications on intention to use e-Government to get a preliminary overview of the
\par
factors that determine usage intention. They have found that the adaption of the
\par
constructs usefulness and ease of use are predominant in the literature of usage
\par
intention of e-Government. Additionally, they have analyzed conceptual and practice-
\par
oriented contributions to expand the findings of empirical research. Here, they have
\par
found that the most frequently applied determinants are Personalization, Currentness of
\par
Public Information and Services, and the Integration of Social Media and Mobile
\par
Services.2 In order to adequately develop the hypotheses in the next subsection, the
\par
authors have tentatively added the determinants identified in terms of the empirical and
\par
conceptual literature analysis for further adjustments and verifications.
\par
Regarding the second stage, the reason for doing the benchmarking is not only because
\par
it enriches the conceptualization by bringing in practical examples (Creswell and Zhang
\par
2009), but also because it facilitates the identification of emerging trends. Moreover, the
\par
decision to apply a case study approach was further driven by the fact that e-Government--
\par
being in an early stage and developing fast-- is a complex phenomenon characterized by
\par
heterogeneous adoption behavior of citizens of different municipalities (Venkatesh et al.
\par
2012). In this context, a case study is an appropriate approach to reflect this complex
\par
phenomenon and enable theory building (Eisenhardt 1989). In particular, the authors have
\par
therefore conducted a qualitative website content analysis referring to the content-related
\par
measuring instruments from Fassnacht and Koese (2006). On this basis, they have
\par
compared the international city portal cases with regard to their service width and service
\par
depth. Such qualitative research method is more reasonable than focus groups or inter-
\par
views. This is because it facilitates conducting relevant factors that reproduce the reality of
\par
the service offer of a city portal and reflect the need satisfaction of the individuals. A
\par
further advantage over other qualitative research methods is that it reduces self-fulfilling
\par
prophecies, as it is conducted using observations in a context that is not produced by the
\par
researcher (Kozinets 1998). The benchmarking results indicate that the stage of maturity
\par
considerably differs among the city portals. The e-Government portal of the city of New
\par
