\par
Determinants of Intention to use e-Government City Portals
\par
Full Online Service Use For the following factor description, see also Wirtz and Kurtz
\par
(2016). A core indicator to measure the process of e-Government service delivery is the
\par
Bfull online availability\textasciicircum{} of public e-Services, which was firstly introduced in the
\par
framework of the five-stage maturity model in the benchmark study by Capgemini
\par
and the European Commission in 2001. In this context, e-Government portals offering
\par
Full Online Services enable citizens to handle certain cases fully electronically without
\par
having to appear in person at the competent public authority. Moreover, they allow
\par
portal users to perform public transactional e-Services from a single point of access
\par
within a completely and exclusively online interaction (Al-Sebie and Irani 2005). The
\par
benchmarking analysis shows that the best practice examples such as New York
\par
consider the offer of Full Online Services as a key factor of the e-Government strategy
\par
of modern public administrations. However, Capgemini et al. (2013) have pointed out
\par
that the maturity stage of full online availability strongly differs among countries.
\par
Therefore, the implementation of Full Online Services should be fostered as these kind
\par
of e-Services offering several benefits - primarily including effort, cost, and time-
\par
savings - equally advantaged governments and citizens (Al-Sebie 2014; Capgemini et
\par
al. 2013). Al-Sebie (2014) report a lack of studies that integrate transactional and
\par
integrative services involving Full Online Services. Existing studies do not empiricallyDeterminants of Citizen Usage Intentions in e-Government 359
\par
test the influence of citizens' Full Online Service use on the intention to use local e-
\par
Government portals (Assar et al. 2011). They rather solely propose conceptual frame-
\par
works regarding this topic to understand the maturity of e-Government (ibid). There-
\par
fore, this study suggests that citizens' Full Online Service use may influence the
\par
intention to use e-Government city portals. Thus, we formulate the following
\par
hypothesis:
\par
H1: Citizens' Full Online Service Use will positively influence citizens' intention
\par
to use e-Government city portals.
\par
Social Media Integration For the following factor description, see also Wirtz and
\par
Kurtz (2016). In recent years, not only profit-oriented companies but also public
\par
administration authorities have recognized the advantages of social media (Linders
\par
2012). AsBsocial media provides a powerful platform to help government communi-
\par
cate directly with constituents and be more visible on the Web\textasciicircum{} (Waseda University
\par
2013, p. 12), the number of countries making use of social media applications for e-
\par
Government service offers has almost quadrupled from 2010 to 2014 (United Nations
\par
2014). According to the present study's benchmarking of leading international city
\par
portals, the official website of the city of New York, for instance, integrates manifold
\par
social media links in 312 social media channels that are distributed across 12 different
\par
applications and platforms, such as Facebook, Twitter, Tumblr, Flickr, YouTube, etc.
\par
In the light of these recent developments, the integration of social media into e-
\par
Government city portals is about to transform public authority processes fundamentally
\par
(Lee and Kwak 2012; Bryer 2011). This is because it promotes the engagement of
\par
citizens regarding administrative processes, generates higher levels of transparency, and
\par
improves citizens-to-government relationships (Bons\'on et al. 2012). There are ground-
\par
breaking possibilities of e-Government service provision via social media applications.
\par
Simultaneously citizen's interest increasingly grows due to changed online user habits
\par
(Bons\'on et al. 2015; Morgeson et al. 2011). Therefore, the integration of social media
\par
into local e-Government city portals may be a crucial factor in determining intention to
\par
use. Hence, this study proposes the following hypothesis:
\par
H2: Social Media Integration into e-Government city portals will positively
\par
influence citizens' intention to use e-Government city portals.
\par
Mobile Service Integration Beside the ubiquity of social media, the smartphone has
\par
become an omnipresent device in everyday citizen life. In the year 2014, the number of
\par
global smartphone users reached 2 billion and is expected to increase by a further 22 \%
\par
to 2.5 billion in 2015 (Strategy Analytics 2014). Given that simultaneously the number
\par
of countries offering official government mobile applications and mobile portals has
\par
doubled from 2012 to 2014, policymakers should considerB[\dots ] planning for mobile
\par
web and mobile apps for the next wave of mobile users with smartphones and tablets\textasciicircum{}
\par
(United Nations 2014, p. 100). Both practitioners and scholars have become aware of
\par
the great importance of m-Government for citizens using public services, since it has
\par
the potential to deliver real-time and location-based information on demand, render360 B.W. Wirtz, O.T. Kurtz
\par
transactional e-Services, and reach citizens mobile to provide innovative customized
\par
services (Al-Hujran 2012).
\par
In the light of these advantages and although m-Government implementation is still
\par
in its very early stages (Al-Hujran 2012), some more developed e-Governments have
\par
incrementally expanded the provision of mobile services on their city portals. The
\par
results of the present study's benchmarking show that the local e-Government website
\par
of Hong Kong, for example, provides 76 official mobile apps that offer information and
\par
services in all different categories, e.g. includingBHealth \& Medical Services\textasciicircum{} and
\par
BCulture, Leisure \& Sports\textasciicircum. This current transition from e-Government to m-
\par
Government calls for research on m-Government integration since a matchless inte-
\par
gration of governmental m-Services has not been achieved yet (Al Thunibat et al.
\par
2011). Therefore, citizens may be faced with issues of quality and usage acceptance
\par
regarding public m-Services (Zamzami and Mahmud 2012). Since the successful
\par
adoption of m-Government by citizens is not adequately researched yet (Shareef
\par
et al. 2012), this study proposes that integrating mobile services into local e-
\par
Government portals may influence usage intention, thus leading us to the following
\par
hypothesis:
\par
H3: Mobile Service Integration into e-Government city portals will positively
\par
influence citizens' intention to use e-Government city portals.
\par
Personalized User Account The e-Government trend has also moved toward a high
\par
level of service personalization or customization at the individual level, enabling
\par
citizens toBpull\textasciicircum{} the services they desire via their preferred channel and toBdesign\textasciicircum{}
\par
their own unique service portfolio or dashboard (United Nations 2014). City portal
\par
officers may implement personalized e-Government services in the e-Government city
\par
portal itself and in a mobile version. They may enable citizens to create user accounts as
\par
the example of the city portal of Hong Kong in the benchmarking analysis shows,
\par
which provides a personalized user account calledBMyGovHK\textasciicircum. This public e-Service
\par
application allows citizens, for example, to organize their frequently used online
\par
services into a checklist for easy access, to subscribe to government reminders and
\par
notifications through various channels, or view individual government billing accounts
\par
and make online payments (Hong Kong Government 2015). These recent develop-
\par
ments call for investigating the role of personalized e-Government services.
\par
The manifold e-Commerce studies examining web content personalization, includ-
\par
ing features such as complex catalog navigation and product customization, show that
\par
the personalized experience enhances online performance and increases customer
\par
satisfaction, loyalty, and sales (e.g., Thongpapanl and Rehman Ashraf 2011; Liao et
\par
al. 2005). However, scholars also found that the management of websites and service
\par
personalization should take user's privacy concerns into account (e.g., Lee et al. 2011;
\par
Sheng et al. 2008). In contrast to the valuable, empirical results regarding personaliza-
\par
tion in e-Commerce, scientists have not yet adequately researched the role of person-
\par
alization in the context of e-Government (van Velsen et al. 2015). Thus, the results of
\par
service personalization within the field of e-Commerce are not readily transferable to
\par
the e-Government context. However, research on e-Government service personalization
\par
has produced scant findings indicating that personalization of e-Services in the publicDeterminants of Citizen Usage Intentions in e-Government 361
\par
sector offers great opportunities to improve communication and positively influence the
\par
intention to use online content personalization for e-Government (ibid). Nevertheless,
\par
scholars also suggest that e-Governments need to manage user obstacles, such as
\par
access, trust, control, and privacy regarding personalized e-Government (Pieterson et
\par
al. 2007). These conflictive findings indicate that further research on personalized e-
\par
Government is required. The influence of personalization on citizens' intention to use
\par
the web portal is therefore an appropriate indicator for the effectiveness of personalized
\par
e-Government web content. In the light of these observations, web content and service
\par
personalization for e-Government city portals-- in terms of offering an individual and
\par
personalized user account-- may be a crucial factor in determining intention to use.
\par
H4: A Personalized User Account at e-Government city portals will positively
\par
influence citizens' intention to use e-Government city portals.
\par
E-Participation Service Integration An e-Government portal determines its effectiv-
\par
ity by offering citizens a comprehensive access to their government and by facilitating a
\par
more inclusive contact (Gulati et al. 2014). This may be achieved through the involve-
\par
ment of citizens in policy processes (Roman and Miller 2013) and citizen participation
\par
as a means of collaborating with citizens to promote democratic values (Wirtz and
\par
Daiser 2015; Kim and Lee 2012). Regarding developed e-Government projects, the
\par
extension of ITC into citizen involvement and participation is called e-Participation
\par
(Zheng et al. 2014). Many municipalities have incrementally established e-Participation
\par
services at their e-Government portals without meeting expectations and building a
\par
critical mass (Themistocleous et al. 2012). In addition, the results of the benchmarking
\par
in the present study show that the city portals considered are in different maturity stages
\par
with regard to the implementation of e-Participation services. However, internationally
\par
the city portals tend to promote citizens' e-Participation since especially the city portal
\par
of New York increasingly offers manifold e-Participation services. Most scholars agree
\par
that the literature has left significant gaps in our knowledge of the relationships among
\par
the management of e-Participation services and citizens' experiences of e-Participation
\par
(e.g., Feeney and Welch 2012; Kim and Lee 2012). Since insights about the effect of
\par
integrating e-Participation services into e-Government websites on usage intention may
\par
help to fill these gaps, this study presents the following hypothesis:
\par
H5: E-Participation Service Integration into e-Government city portals will posi-
\par
tively influence citizens' intention to use.
\par
Currentness of Public Information and Services In an early study investigating the
\par
quality of an IS, King and Epstein (1976) quote that an information is only valuable
\par
when it reduces uncertainty. Therefore, a number of factors including currentness-- as
\par
an important attribute of website quality-- need to be addressed in designing the
\par
information component of a website (e.g., Lee and Kozar 2006; Smith 2001). In this
\par
context, Dragulanescu (2001) uses currentness as a criterion to evaluate websites and
\par
defines it as the degree to which the distributed information is belonging to the present.
\par
Lakeworth (2008) expands and specifies the definition by describing currentness as362 B.W. Wirtz, O.T. Kurtz
\par
accuracy of information enhanced by the correctness and relevance of changing
\par
information.
\par
Along with the rapid development of ICT and the fact that e-Government portals
\par
now offer a broad range of services, including diverse governmental information, data,
\par
statistics, and other public e-Services, the currentness of public information and
\par
services is regarded as one of the standard guidelines for the web design of govern-
\par
mental agencies (Erkollar and Oberer 2010). When designing and maintaining an e-
\par
Government website, the key objective is toB[\dots ] maximize accuracy and currentness
\par
of information and services\textasciicircum{} (Jafari and Ali 2011, p. 1323). As the currentness of
\par
public information and services is a major quality characteristic of an e-Government
\par
website, this study proposes the following hypothesis:
\par
H6: Currentness of Public Information and Services of e-Government city portals
\par
will positively influence citizens' intention to use e-Government city portals.
\par
Research Model
\par
In terms of the mixed-method approach, this study has identified a set of practice-
\par
oriented determinants. They include e-Government trends as well as innovative public
\par
e-Services on the local level. In testing the above-mentioned hypotheses, we assume
\par
that all determinants positively influence the intention to use e-Government city portals.
\par
These relationships are summarized in the proposed research model presented in Fig. 1.
\par
Compared to higher e-Government levels, insights into citizens' usage intentions are
\par
particularly relevant for the local level of e-Government because the city portals
\par
constitute the focal point of contact for citizens (Piehler et al. 2016). To the best of
\par
our knowledge, no research study up to now has empirically examined usage intentions
\par
Fig. 1 Research model for intention to use e-Government city portalsDeterminants of Citizen Usage Intentions in e-Government 363
\par
of e-Government city portal users, applying determinants that consider these modern
\par
and advanced public e-Services in an integrated model.
\par
Method and Data
\par
This study applies binary logistic regression analysis to investigate intention to use e-
\par
Government city portals since this less restrictive method has developed to an alterna-
\par
tive to the linear discriminant function (Tabachnick and Fidell 2001). This method suits
\par
the purpose of the study well since the outcome variable is dichotomous and predictor
\par
variables are categorical (Field 2005). Accordingly, the response variable used in this
\par
study has two categories that are coded as`high intention users, 1' or `low intention
\par
users, 0'. The aim of the study is to investigate the influence of the determinants
\par
illustrated in the proposed research model (Fig. 1) on the dichotomous outcome (`1' or
\par
`0') of usage intention of e-Government city portals. Therefore, this model estimates
\par
the probabilistic relationships of the variables and the outcome using odds ratio (Wright
\par
1995). The odds ratio indicates the relative effect on the odds of an event by a one-unit
\par
change in the independent variable (Hosmer and Lemeshow 2000). In this context, an
\par
odds ratio greater than 1 implies an increased likelihood, whereas an odds ratio less
\par
than 1 implies a decreased likelihood. According to Khoshgoftaar and Allen (1999),
\par
one advantage of logistic regression analysis is the straightforward interpretation of the
\par
odds ratio. The outcome of binary logistic regression analysis is not the prediction of a
\par
numerical value, as is the case when using linear regression, but the probability of
\par
belonging to one of two conditions (Kleinbaum 1994). Within the binary logistic
\par
regression we have particularly chosen the enter method as procedure for variable
\par
selection. This technique enters all variables as a block in a single step in order to
\par
prevent the omission of any variable that theoretically may influence citizens' usage
\par
intention of e-Government city portals.
\par
The empirical investigation has been conducted in Germany asking all existing 233
\par
small to medium-sized municipalities (i.e. up to 100,000 residents) in the federal state
\par
of Rhineland-Palatinate to participate in a web-based survey (see also Wirtz and Kurtz
\par
2016). This sampling method represents a complete sample approach, in which the set
\par
of objects from the parent population includes all such objects that satisfy the set of the
\par
selection criteria (Cochran 1977). We directly gathered data from e-Government city
\par
portals to ensure that all participants are actual users of e-Government services. A
\par
number of 117 municipalities agreed to take part in the study and placed our survey link
\par
on the front page of their city portals, accounting for over 50 \% of the contacted
\par
municipalities. The data ascertainment generated a sample of 717 citizens who were
\par
separated in low and high intention users according to their responses. This sample size
\par
fulfills all methodical requirements since the recommended sample size for logistic
\par
regression analysis is not less than 100 (Pampel 2000) or a minimum of 50 cases per
\par
independent variable (Wright 1995). The descriptive statistics show that about two
\par
thirds (69.5 \%) of the respondents were male and one-third (30.5 \%) female with an
\par
average age of 46 years. 6.2 \% of the participants have finished secondary modern
\par
school, 22.4 \% have secondary school certificates, 21.7 \% have technical baccalaureate
\par
diploma, 47.8 \% have post-secondary school diploma, and 1.8 \% have other education. The
\par
majority (74.1 \%) of the respondents uses the internet more than 10 hours per week. Before364 B.W. Wirtz, O.T. Kurtz
\par
performing the binary logistic regression analysis, the authors have tested the sample for
\par
potential systematic bias in the data. Therefore, they have calculated the squared
\par
Mahalanobis distances to identify questionnaires that are completed but made no sense
\par
(Hair 2010). The results indicate that all questionnaires deliver meaningful responses.
\par
Furthermore, the authors have checked the data for non-response bias (Ruxton 2006)
\par
comparing the responses of early and late participants. Since late responses did not vary
\par
significantly from early ones, the authors could not confirm a non-response bias in the
\par
sample (Armstrong and Overton 1977)
\par
Empirical Results
\par
The authors have analyzed the data using the enter method of binary logistic regression
\par
to correctly predict usage intention of e-Government city portals. By examining the
\par
collinearity statistics, they have first tested the absence of multicollinearity which is an
\par
important restriction for binary logistic regression. The results indicate the absence of
\par
multicollinearity among the predictor variables since the Variance Inflation Factor (VIF)
\par
is less than 5 and the value for tolerance is above 0.25 for each of the predictor variables
\par
(Belsley et al. 1980). To test the overall significance of the proposed model, we obtained
\par
the model chi-square by means of the omnibus goodness of fit test (Hosmer and
\par
Lemeshow 2000; McCullagh and Nelder 1989). Its results significantly predict that
\par
the model fits accurately ({$\chi$}2 (df = 6) = 91.198; p = .000). In particular, the test shows that
\par
the addition of the independent variables to the model reduces the deviance (-2log) by
\par
91.198 on 6 degrees of freedom, thus implying high variations in usage intention of e-
\par
Government city portals. The p-value is significant (p = .000 {$<$} .05) and shows that the
\par
independent variables explain these variations in intention to use. The most important
\par
and reliable goodness of fit test is the Hosmer and Lemeshow test that verifies the
\par
difference between observed and predicted values. The smaller the difference, the better
\par
the model fit is. Therefore, the test result has to be not significant. Outcomes of the test
\par
indicated that the binary logistic model fits the data as the p-value is 0.464 {$>$} 0.05 (Table
\par
1). The following tables illustrate that the model correctly predicts 66.3 \% of the cases
\par
(Table 2) in comparison to 59.7 \% of the predicted cases excluded from the independent
\par
variables. Overall, 81.1 \% of the respondents who have indicated that they are high
\par
intention users of e-Government city portal have been correctly predicted (Table 2).
\par
The logistic regression analysis confirms four of the six hypotheses suggested. In
\par
particular, the results presented in Table 3 indicate that all significant factors of the
