Discussion of Findings, Implications, and Conclusions
\par
By applying binary logistic regression analysis, this study seeks to correctly predict the
\par
intention to use e-Government city portals. The model indicates that four of the six
\par
proposed hypotheses are supported showing significant positive effects on usage
\par
intention. The findings of the present analysis enable public administration officials
\par
to draw some valuable practical implications for the successful management of e-
\par
Government city portals. In the early stages of planning and implementing e-
\par
Government projects, decision makers of local e-Governments need to manage the
\par
service provision of specific public e-Services orderly and foresighted. For this reason,
\par
portal management should follow some important advices to ensure e-Government
\par
portal success from the very beginning. First, it has to guarantee that all e-Services
\par
offered and material provided such as general information, data, statistics, forms, etc.
\par
deliver current and timely information. The Currentness of Public Information and
\par
Services should be considered as a fundamental task that needs to be first priority and
\par
constantly managed when designing and maintaining e-Government websites. Second,
\par
the study's content and outcomes emphasize the importance of a user-centric strategy
\par
for designing and implementing e-Government services at the local level. Therefore,
\par
instead of giving their sole attention to the technological implementation, e-
\par
Governments should also place special emphasis on the user. In this context, the present
\par
analysis demonstrates that determinants of intention to use a city portal are strongly
\par
associated with the integration of innovative public e-Services that reflect the changed
\par
needs of citizens. When designing and implementing public e-Services, public author-
\par
ities of city portals shall therefore first benchmark their city portals with best practice
\par
examples and then transfer specific concepts if applicable. In a second step, they should
\par
analyze and anticipate current online user habits by means of periodical citizen surveys
\par
and focus groups that enable the direct dialogue and active cooperation between lead
\par
users and public administrations. In that way, decision makers are able to identify e-
\par
Government trends to offer up-to-date and user-oriented public e-Services that enhance
\par
city portal usage.
\par
In particular, the present study has shown that offering public services that enable
\par
citizens to handle their concerns fully electronically without having to appear in person
\par
at the competent public authority positively influences intention to use e-Government
\par
city portals. The analysis has further demonstrated that citizens who are integrated into
\par
public administration processes of their municipalities have an increased intention to
\par
use city portals. Given the growing political demand by democratic governments for a
\par
stronger involvement of citizens in political processes, e-Government city portals
\par
should consider to develop an integral strategy for citizen e-Participation. Therefore,
\par
the authors suggest portal management to expand all dimensions of e-Participation
\par
including e-Information, e-Consultation, and e-Decision-making. The different kinds of
\par
e-Participation services may include citizen participation in political decisions, such as
\par
financial and budgetary issues or the submission of an online application that, for
\par
example, integrates citizens into certain city planning projects or serves as an online
\par
complaint system (e.g., the report of damaged sidewalks). Furthermore, a modern
\par
public administration should expand its mobile government strategy due to citizens'
\par
ubiquitous use of mobile devices and the advantages of integrating mobile services into
\par
e-Government city portals for both public administrations and citizens. City portalsDeterminants of Citizen Usage Intentions in e-Government 367
\par
should offer official mobile apps that provide informational e-Services, such as opening
\par
hours, status requests regarding individual public concerns, local traffic reports, or local
\par
event information. At the same time, the authors recommend to deliver communicative
\par
and transactional e-Services, such as mobile arrangement of appointments or mobile
\par
parking ticket sales. Another possibility to commit citizens to use e-Government
\par
services is the integration of user accounts, since this facilitates citizens to design their
\par
personal and unique service portfolio. In this context, they may review and organize
\par
their individual public concerns by means of a dashboard that provides real-time
\par
information. Furthermore, Social Media Integration into e-Government city portals
\par
may offer manifold opportunities ranging from personalization to e-Participation.
\par
Specifically, it has the potential to improve the relationship between citizens and public
\par
administrations by enhancing communication, interaction, transparency, and participa-
\par
tion (Bons\'on et al. 2012). Despite the model's rejection of Personalized User Account
\par
and Social Media Integration, the authors recommend public authorities to maintain the
\par
path of integrating social media and personalized web elements into e-Government city
\par
portals. At least they should be prepared for the varying requirements of citizens and
\par
the rising importance of implementing these kind of innovative public e-Services in the
\par
future.
\par
The proposed model further provides new insights for scientists as the applied
\par
practice-oriented approach reflects the reality more than the sole observation of IS
\par
characteristics (e.g., system quality). Therefore, it delivers appropriate and useful
\par
results for further examinations. Moreover, the present study-- being detached from
\par
the e-Commerce and technology-oriented perspective-- offers an integrated model of
\par
usage intention that specifically considers the e-Government context using specific
\par
innovative public e-Services. Scientist should increasingly neglect the supply perspec-
\par
tive and should foster user-centric research. The authors recommend that upcoming
\par
research considers current and future trends and involves specific e-Service types rather
\par
than generic and abstract constructs. The study shows that scientific discussions can
\par
mainly achieve a reorientation towards a choice of model approaches that take account
\par
of citizen needs and the specific e-Government context by applying a mixed-method
\par
approach instead of a purely quantitative one. In this way, scientists can provide
\par
appropriate and reasonable implications for practice since a service and user-oriented
\par
portal management is reliant on recommendations with regard to the implementation of
\par
specific public e-Services. In particular, future research should further expand our
\par
results regarding the full online availability of public services as well as citizens'
\par
intentions toward e-Participation and public mobile services. Furthermore, future
\par
research endeavors should examine the influence of personalized user accounts and
\par
social media services on the success of city portals. These attempts should involve an
\par
appropriate sample that contains users of modern city portals with high maturity stages
\par
to provide implications based on best practice examples.
\par
Besides the scientific contribution and managerial implications, the present study
\par
also comprises limitations regarding different aspects. One issue is that the proposed
\par
model has rejected the influence of a Personalized User Account (H4) and of Social
\par
Media Integration (H2). On the one hand, this may be due to the novelty of this kind of
\par
e-Government service offer or, on the other hand, may be caused by a rather tradition-
\par
alist sample including elder e-Government users (only 14.2 \% of the respondents were
\par
under 30 years old). In identifying relevant determinants of e-Government city portal368 B.W. Wirtz, O.T. Kurtz
\par
usage intention, this study only considers a limited period and hence does not cover a
\par
long-term period of time. Considering data ascertainment, this study is limited to the
\par
examination of public e-Services in German municipalities and cannot fully eliminate a
\par
potential self-selection bias. Moreover, since the authors have carried out the survey in
\par
Germany, the results are not necessarily generalizable to e-Government portals outside
\par
of Germany. Since culture-specific differences may be relevant with regard to user
\par
habits and requirements or the implementation of certain public e-Services, a cross-
\par
cultural examination seems to be advisable for future research. However, since the
\par
present study is unique in providing a scientific contribution for the intention to use city
\par
portals regarding the implementation of specific innovative e-Government services, the
\par
authors hope that public officials benefit from the study's implications for an efficient
\par
portal management.
