\textbf{Abstract:}
\par
Efficient decisions along the supply chain have traditionally demanded sophisticated
\par
information sharing processes. Even with decades of research on theoretical and practical
\par
developments on integrating systems and stakeholders, in practice, we still seem to struggle to
\par
achieve full transparency and mitigate inefficiency challenges. We explore the emerging
\par
sentiment analysis technique to augment sales and operations planning (S\&OP) with currently
\par
unavailable exogenous information. Even though sentiment analysis has gained traction, a
\par
comprehensive application in supply chains has not yet been attempted. Relevant topics are
\par
reviewed to allow an examination of the key relationships in a process framework, grounded
\par
in dual-process and bullwhip effect theory. Our proposed conceptual framework extends our
\par
conception of sentiment analysis integration to improve supply chain decisions and
\par
performance. The framework addresses managers interested in developing additional analytical
\par
capabilities and researchers to initiate further empirical research on the potential held by
\par
sentiment analysis in supply chain research.
