\par
sharing, collaboration.This is the \textbf{post-print} (i.e., it is the authors' final draft, post-refereeing, and is therefore the authors' accepted manuscript) of the article:
\par
Wood, L. C., Reiners, T., \& Srivastava, H. S. (in press). Think exogenous to excel: Alternative supply chain data to improve transparency and decisions. \emph{International Journal of}
\par
\emph{Logistics: Research and Applications}
\par
\textbf{1. Introduction}
\par
The increasing importance of supply chain management within broader management practices
\par
has enabled firms to focus more closely on customers' expectations and needs. Meeting
\par
customers' needs is achieved through improved management of materials, information, and
\par
financial flows between firms with the intent of supplying the right product to the right place
\par
at the right time in the right quantity and right specification (Mentzer et al., 2001). In addition
\par
to the physical movement of materials to markets, the second function of supply chains is the
\par
market mediation, ensuring the correct mixture of products makes it to market (Fisher, 1997).
\par
Both the transformation-and-transport and market mediation functions require and rely on the
\par
transfer and use of various forms of both timely and accurate information, including inventory
\par
locations and transport options (physical movement) as well as the understanding of market
\par
demand and consumer desires (market mediation). Due to the requirement for information,
\par
information sharing and system integration have long been perceived as a fundamental
\par
requirement for successful supply chain management (Lee, Padmanabhan, \& Wang, 1997).
\par
Contemporary information sharing approaches underpin collaboration and cooperation
\par
along the supply chain (Cheng \& Wu, 2005; Li, Yan, Wang, \& Xia, 2005). A primary method
\par
involves the sharing of point-of-sale (POS) data to indicate what is happening in the
\par
marketplace. POS data sharing allows information about consumer preference to be shared by
\par
other firms in the supply chain and may support decision-making activities. The importance of
\par
POS data sharing as an essential element of collaboration over the supply chain has been
\par
underscored by many scholars. POS data sharing allows even a firm distant from the market to
\par
gauge changes and shifts in preferences and in addition to that, to deliver a superior
\par
performance through improved chain coordination (de Treville, Shapiro, \& Hameri, 2004; Lee
\par
\& Whang, 2000; Sahin \& Robinson, 2002). Here, we consider supply chain performance in
\par
relation to supply chain activities, addressing the end-customer needs and requirements
\par
independent of industry-specifics or operational and strategic orientation.
\par
The abovementioned is an expected, traditional, even conservative, and textbook approach
\par
to supply chain management. To grasp the need of a paradigm change in order to use exogenous
\par
information we have to understand current mechanisms and implications that an upstream
\par
supplier has to determine, and evaluate the downstream market demand changes that are often
\par
underdeveloped and fraught with problems. Scholars including Stank et al. (2015) and Waller
\par
and Fawcett (2013) prognose a shift away from a culture of hoarding information and passing
\par
only processed fragments to suppliers towards a culture of improved visibility of (big) data
\par
from markets and internal stakeholders as well as towards gaining coordination vertically over
\par
the supply chain. While Stank et al. (2015) consider the stronger independence of suppliers and an
\par
overall value increase of the supply chain as a major benefit, their suggestions will be challenging
\par
to implement as the system still requires humans -- that may cheat or make mistakes -- and may not
\par
always generate outcomes in the best interest of the supply chain. Furthermore, traction for the
\par
concepts would require the cumulative development of existing systems and management
\par
structures.
\par
``Doing more of the same'' regarding upgrading systems will, in this paradigm, still
\par
struggle to overcome the issues relating to trust between partners and doubts about supply chain
\par
transparency.This is the \textbf{post-print} (i.e., it is the authors' final draft, post-refereeing, and is therefore the authors' accepted manuscript) of the article:
\par
Wood, L. C., Reiners, T., \& Srivastava, H. S. (in press). Think exogenous to excel: Alternative supply chain data to improve transparency and decisions. \emph{International Journal of}
\par
\emph{Logistics: Research and Applications}
\par
On the other hand, it is a common issue to believe that one can effectively gain market insight
\par
from surveys. However, any survey is resource-intensive, timely, costly to conduct, and will
\par
suffer challenges in the sampling control that make many surveys non-representative
\par
(Parasuraman, Grewal, \& Krishnan, 2006), resulting in often inaccurate market-based demand
\par
causing an over- or under-supplying of the market. Costs associated with these excess
\par
inventories within the food sector have been estimated as being as high as 12.5-25\% or 75-
\par
100b of 300b value (Lee, Padmanabhan, \& Whang, 2004) and these costs impact on many
\par
members of the chain. Furthermore, upstream capacity decisions are made by the perceived
\par
strength or long-term success of a given market; therefore, failing to understand the market
\par
changes can lead to expensive capacity management mismatches upstream (Chen \& Lee,
\par
2012). Indeed, the way in which the costs of mismatches fall disproportionately on upstream
\par
members increases the imperative on these firms to prevent errors (Ma, Wang, Che, Huang, \&
\par
Xu, 2013). Therefore, upstream suppliers using sentiment analysis to receive timely updates to
\par
market demand changes can improve their performance.
\par
We take an unbiased look at the market- or demand-driven nature of supply chains with a
\par
focus on the generally endogenous information sharing along supply chains. The traditional
\par
use of endogenous data is then compared to advancing the use of exogenus data analytics for
\par
comprehensive decision-making support. Data analytics on social media data is a growing
\par
research area in supply chain management, yet the implications that this has in light of the (theory
\par
of) the bullwhip effect are still unexplored. This conceptual research paper proposes a framework
\par
to fill this gap by pursuing two major objectives. The first objective is to combine our knowledge
\par
of existing supply chain approaches that aim to improve supply chain performance by improved
\par
planning and execution, with a focus on the role that `sentiment analysis' may play in this situation.
\par
The second objective is to apply sentiment analysis to develop an integrated process framework to
\par
improve supply chain performance. In developing this framework, we draw on the theory of the
\par
bullwhip effect (BWE; a.k.a. demand amplification) and `dual-process theory'.
\par
In this conceptual paper, we identify and explore the emerging phenomenon of sentiment
\par
analysis to augment sales and operations planning (S\&OP) with exogenous information
\par
currently not available along the supply chain. Thus, we first connect sentiment analysis to the
\par
concept of demand-sensing in supply chain management. Second, we briefly outline the main
\par
techniques of sentiment analysis and how these analytic approaches can be used to gauge
\par
changes in the sentiment of product reviews online. Third, we propose a conceptual framework
\par
for an effective integration of sentiment analysis to improve supply chain decision and
\par
performance. Finally, we discuss limitations and opportunities of the framework for
\par
practitioners to develop additional analytical capabilities and for empirical researchers to
\par
conduct experiments to validate its effectiveness.
\par
\textbf{2. The role of demand information in supply chain decisions}
\par
Supply chain management has key objectives relating to ensuring the products are in the right
\par
locations at the right time and in the right quantity and specification. Accomplishing these
\par
objectives requires a level of transparency regarding the activities undertaken amongst
\par
members of the supply chain coupled with comprehension about the product characteristicsThis is the \textbf{post-print} (i.e., it is the authors' final draft, post-refereeing, and is therefore the authors' accepted manuscript) of the article:
\par
Wood, L. C., Reiners, T., \& Srivastava, H. S. (in press). Think exogenous to excel: Alternative supply chain data to improve transparency and decisions. \emph{International Journal of}
\par
\emph{Logistics: Research and Applications}
\par
and quantities that should be delivered to specific markets. Throughout the rest of the
\par
discussion we will use the term `marketplace demand' to denote the actual demand from
\par
consumers, the end-users of the product; `retailer' will mean the firm selling directly to the
\par
consumers; and `supplier' will mean a firm in the supply chain that is manufacturing
\par
components, subassemblies, or final products which are yet to be transformed into the finished
\par
product or physically shifted to the retailers' premises (i.e., this includes manufacturing and
\par
also distributors/wholesalers). The `tier' reflects the distance of a supplier from the marketplace
\par
with \emph{1} being closest and \emph{n} being most distant (see Figure 1).
\par
\textbf{Figure 1. Supply chain showing flow of information and materials/products}
\par
The fundamental premise is that more access to better information about the market demand
\par
can be used to enhance the decisions made by a single supplier in the chain. In this way, we
\par
follow the theory of the bullwhip effect, or demand amplification, where demand order
\par
variability increases as orders are passed upstream away from the consumer (Hau L. Lee,
\par
Padmanabhan, \& Whang, 1997). This theory states that information sharing results in
\par
information transmission upstream, with suppliers responding to information that has been
\par
distorted by the ``repetitive processing of consumption data'' (Lee et al., 1997 p. 99). A key
\par
method to reduce the BWE is through ensuring that information from downstream is passed
\par
upstream quickly (Lee et al., 1997). Despite recognising that technological aspects to facilitate
\par
sharing of information upstream have been improved, Croson and Donohue (2006) undertook
\par
two controlled experiments to investigate the impact of behavioural causes (decision bias) on
\par
demand amplification after providing demand distribution visibility to the participants. They
\par
found that the bullwhip effect still existed even after inventory information was available to
\par
the participants in real time. These inconsistencies can be explained by incorporating the
\par
concept of `bounded rationality'
\par
. For example, while it is generally assumed that supply chain
\par
partners are perfectly rational (and, for example, can choose optimal stocking levels that can
\par
yield maximum profit), experimental studies have confirmed that human decision-makers fail
\par
to optimise inventory as theory suggests (Su, 2008). In addition, Nienhaus et al. (2006)
\par
identified `safe harbour' and `panic' as strategies that encourage extreme behaviours that
\par
contribute to the bullwhip effect. When participants in their study attempted the `safe harbour'
\par
strategy, they ordered more to increase the safety stock levels. When they utilised the `panic'
\par
strategy, they strived to reduce the safety stock levels and then struggled with the increase in
\par
end-consumers' demand. Both extreme cases contributed to the bullwhip effect and affectedThis is the \textbf{post-print} (i.e., it is the authors' final draft, post-refereeing, and is therefore the authors' accepted manuscript) of the article:
\par
Wood, L. C., Reiners, T., \& Srivastava, H. S. (in press). Think exogenous to excel: Alternative supply chain data to improve transparency and decisions. \emph{International Journal of}
\par
\emph{Logistics: Research and Applications}
\par
the supply chain negatively. Therefore, behavioural elements and poor decision-making
\par
capabilities can lead to reduction in the effectiveness of decisions (Sterman, 1989).
\par
Based on above discussion, firms' supply chain performance can, therefore, be improved
\par
with earlier access to marketplace information. A firm that can better predict demand signals
\par
and adjust production or distribution accordingly in a more proactive manner should have an
\par
advantage over competitors (Lau, 2012). This `strategic fit' between the operational decisions
\par
and the environmental variables (i.e., marketplace demand changes, in this case) reduces the
\par
impact of inefficiencies or costs, such as excess demand, that can accumulate due to the BWE
\par
(Stonebraker \& Liao, 2006).
\par
Retailers have traditionally had a good understanding of changes in demand, market
\par
changes, and shifts in consumer preference. The ability to sell and promote products to the
\par
market was often based on this ability to `sense' what was desired by the market and shape
\par
consumer demand accordingly. Successful retailers, who understand and know how to
\par
influence the market, often hold a strong bargaining position in supply chains; for example,
\par
Wal-Mart's dominance has allowed it to dictate terms, such as requiring the top 100 suppliers
\par
to rapidly roll-out radio-frequency identification (RFID) tagging (Fontanella, 2004). More
\par
recently, other firms (distant from the market) have increasingly seen the value in the ability to
\par
engage in `demand sensing' activities (Folinas \& Rabi, 2012). Awareness of overall demand,
\par
which the supply chains seeks to fulfil, should support a strongly demand-driven approach,
\par
whereby the supply chain output becomes well-aligned to market requirements. Therefore,
\par
demand-sensing solutions should lead to market-focused outcomes with benefits to the firms
\par
involved (Folinas \& Rabi, 2012)
\par
The challenge, therefore, is to access and use data regarding demand in the marketplace.
\par
Most commonly, this is based on the retailer collecting the POS data indicating sales in the
\par
market and then transferring this to their suppliers. The use of POS data provides
\par
visibility/transparency to both the supplier and retailer whereby benefits may extend beyond
\par
the first tier by using other technologies such as RFID and Electronic Data Interchange (EDI).
\par
Cross-organisation use of RFID allows firms to manage and track the information relating to
\par
the movement and management of stock over multiple tiers and facilities that may be distant
\par
to the retailer. However, this requires connections between firms' ERP systems or the use of
\par
separate, third-party platforms. Similarly, while POS data sharing can expand visibility over
\par
multiple tiers, this is challenging to implement where there is not an existing trading
\par
relationship with the end retailer. Also, in both cases, it is more likely that large firms are more
\par
amenable to implementing these solutions. With such capabilities, a firm upstream will be
\par
capable of sensing shifts in market demand and proactively preparing for these.
\par
Supporting this is the concept of `visibility' of activities in the supply chain. Visibility has
\par
been connected to improved performance and may be enhanced with technology or with
\par
specific inter-organisational initiatives (Bartlett, Julien, \& Baines, 2007). Identification and
\par
recording of stock movements is an essential method to improve intra-firm visibility and has
\par
been based on barcoding or RFID technology, where captured item data can be stored in the
\par
ERP and used in decision-making.This is the \textbf{post-print} (i.e., it is the authors' final draft, post-refereeing, and is therefore the authors' accepted manuscript) of the article:
\par
Wood, L. C., Reiners, T., \& Srivastava, H. S. (in press). Think exogenous to excel: Alternative supply chain data to improve transparency and decisions. \emph{International Journal of}
\par
\emph{Logistics: Research and Applications}
\par
Inter-firm visibility received a significant boost from the application of several related ICT
\par
technologies. First, and perhaps most notably, was the introduction of electronic data
\par
interchange (EDI), one of the first technologies that enabled trading partners to transmit data
\par
accurately at speed (Attaran \& Attaran, 2007). Summarising this research stream, Hazen and
\par
Byrd (2012, p. 8) maintain that the technology ``promotes enhanced levels of effectiveness,
\par
efficiency, and resiliency for the adopting firm''
\par
. EDI enables the transfer of information so
\par
that what happens at another tier can be responded to by the focal firm.
\par
Other management techniques have less reliance in terms of technological solutions. Key
\par
methods include just-in-time II (JIT II) (Dixon, 1997); stock replenishment programmes,
\par
consignment stocks, and vendor-managed inventory (VMI) (\c Cetinkaya \& Lee, 2000); and
\par
collaborative planning forecasting and replenishment (CPFR) (\c Cetinkaya \& Lee, 2000). Each
\par
of these enables the upstream partner to undertake a greater role in managing the materials flow
\par
to maintain superior service levels (Disney \& Towill, 2003), based on improved visibility over
\par
a limited scope of the supply chain. Improved visibility leads to better internally focused
\par
decisions.
\par
In each of these cases, the emphasis is on a dyadic information sharing arrangement in a
\par
way that a particular firm has improved the visibility of supply chain operations. Delays in the
\par
transmission of information upstream along the supply chain have previously been identified
\par
as a key cause of the BWE (Zhang, 2004). Other factors contributing to the BWE include
\par
demand forecast, short lead times, price fluctuations, and promotions (Cachon, Randall, \&
\par
Schmidt, 2007; H. L. Lee et al., 1997).
\par
In contrast to these often dyadic approaches, sentiment analysis allows an upstream firm to
\par
access market-based demand data and can, therefore, be used to improve the upstream firm's
\par
supply chain performance. Many of these dyadic approaches ensure earlier access to
\par
information about the market or changes that are relevant to the suppliers. The buzz associated
\par
with product success and failure can be reflected in social media almost instantly and can be
\par
concurrent with actual retail sales. Furthermore, sentiment analysis can also pick up growing
\par
interest (or waning interest) in particular products, ahead of actual changes in retail sales.
\par
Therefore, it provides early access to data supporting forecasting decisions that will create a
\par
positive effect on supply chain performance by enabling proactive changes to be made to
\par
capacity and inventory management. Market demand shifts can precede sales shifts, indicating
\par
a final delay in information transfer along the chain.
\par
Sentiment analysis is a sub-domain of big data analytics and has become one of the dominant
\par
approaches to address the volume, variety, and velocity in social media, that is, qualitative data
\par
on platforms like Twitter and Facebook (Waller \& Fawcett, 2013). Big data itself became a
\par
buzzword in its role as the consequent extension of data warehouses, which primarily operate
\par
on structured data. While big data has created new challenges (e.g., data growth, infrastructure,
\par
governance/policy, integration, compliance/regulation or visualization), it offers an
\par
opportunity for data scientists to develop predictive analytic techniques and discover unknown
\par
dependencies across various data sources both within and exogenous to the supply chain
\par
(Rozados \& Tjahjono, 2014). Common examples are traffic analytics for transport (Khazaei,
\par
Zareian, Veleda, \& Litoiu, 2016), Internet of Things (O'Leary, 2013), consumer reviewThis is the \textbf{post-print} (i.e., it is the authors' final draft, post-refereeing, and is therefore the authors' accepted manuscript) of the article:
\par
Wood, L. C., Reiners, T., \& Srivastava, H. S. (in press). Think exogenous to excel: Alternative supply chain data to improve transparency and decisions. \emph{International Journal of}
\par
\emph{Logistics: Research and Applications}
\par
analytics and prediction (Chong, Ch'ng, Liu, \& Li, 2015), health care (Bates, Saria, Ohno-
\par
Machado, Shah, \& Escobar, 2014), and social media analysis (Manovich, 2012). As discussed
\par
in the following section, sentiment analysis operates using big data, but specifically it addresses
\par
the emotions or sentiments in online posts, that is, consumers' feelings expressed within their
\par
online reviews. With respect to the scope of this paper, we limit the discussion to the application
\par
of sentiment analysis to improve supply chain performance; for a review on algorithm and
\par
applications see Medhat et al. (2014).
\par
\textbf{3. Sentiment Analysis}
\par
For a single firm, upstream from the market it serves, there is an opportunity to quickly and
\par
directly access data on market-based demand and product acceptance using `sentiment
\par
analysis' or `opinion mining'. Sentiment analysis is based on textual impressions about
\par
products, posted on the Internet. Use of this publicly available data allows a firm to accesses
\par
and use consumer-created data to gauge changes in desires and probable spending patterns.
\par
Although information sharing is crucial in supply chain management, there are several
\par
significant problems. In this section, we outline these practical difficulties and how this limits
\par
the utility of established approaches in terms of information sharing. In particular, we outline
\par
the difficulties of some methods of achieving visibility over more than one tier. Second, we
\par
outline the behavioural changes required. Third, we provide an overview of how a supply chain
\par
change can improve visibility without effective integration.
\par
\textbf{\emph{3.1. Demand sensing with sentiment analysis}}
\par
What, precisely, can we capture with this approach? First, the pipeline of customers can be
\par
inferred and second, we can capture two key pieces of information: the overall impact and the
\par
overall sentiment expressed.
\par
Warren (2008) and Finskud (2009) argue that consumers progress through several
\par
developmental stages over time, creating a `pipeline' of customers at various stages of
\par
awareness and willingness to buy a product. Supply Chain Managers may be most concerned
\par
with the Marketing Managers' evaluation of the overall size and sentiment of the owners and
\par
interested consumers categories, and their overall sentiment of the market, as this will indicate
\par
future sales and supply problems that the firm may face. Together, these data present a Supply
\par
Chain Manager with the opportunity to prepare for changes in the market, in anticipation of
\par
future changes in orders from the customers that reflect the market-based changes. Figure 2
\par
depicts the consumer development stages regarding the change of communication about the
\par
product. With increasing awareness and interest for a product, the consumer passes through
\par
various stages; from not being aware of a product to becoming the owner of it. During these
\par
stages, the communication evolves from non-existing to asking a question in support of making
\par
a decision towards buying and owning the product.This is the \textbf{post-print} (i.e., it is the authors' final draft, post-refereeing, and is therefore the authors' accepted manuscript) of the article:
\par
