\par
\emph{Logistics: Research and Applications}
\par
\textbf{Figure 2. Communication change of consumers in their process of developing an awareness and interest in a product}
\par
\textbf{to become an owner but also contribute to the community by reporting on feedback. The figure is based on the}
\par
\textbf{Customer Choice Pipeline (Warren, 2008, pp. 345-356).}
\par
Capturing the analysis of the corpus of comments and reviews of a product can allow evaluation
\par
of the aggregated marketplace opinion and judgement about a given product. A product that is
\par
not making much impact in the market is more likely to be cancelled; such a reduced impact
\par
early on the release of a product may force Supply Managers to scale back their capacity or
\par
ordering commitments that support the production of the item. Overall discussions about
\par
particular features may feed into future product development decisions. If a large number of
\par
`interested consumers' are identified, it may be an indication that large sales may be realised
\par
shortly. The use of sentiment analysis data can indicate surges/plunges in sales for a product,
\par
similarly to how it would be perceived through analysis of the POS data that was captured and
\par
shared down the supply chain.
\par
Ultimately, all supply chains serve end-customers or consumers, who act as the source of
\par
money for the supply chain. If sentiment analysis is to work, then several relationships must
\par
hold (Wood, Reiners, Srivastava, \& Duong, 2014). First, consumers must in practice discuss
\par
products and provide opinions online as this provides the data that must be captured. Second,
\par
these expressed opinions must correlate with sales of that product to consumers. Third, the
\par
opinions must be amenable to automatic processing with judgements made at the conclusion.
\par
\textbf{\emph{3.2. Consumers evaluate products}}
\par
If products excite or disappoint consumers, we would anticipate that the consumers will express
\par
their feelings about the product. Where the opinion is written, online in an open forum, then
\par
the raw data is available to support the sentiment analysis approach.
\par
Consumer opinions expressed online are most likely to occur in the business-to-consumer
\par
(B2C) market where the users of the product are happy to share and comment on their products
\par
or others' products. Consumer reviews are a foundation for the success of social media and
\par
Web 2.0 technologies (e.g., powering Facebook); in particular, Gen X and Y love to share,
\par
particularly with those close to them in their network (Jansen, Sobel, \& Cook, 2010). InThis is the \textbf{post-print} (i.e., it is the authors' final draft, post-refereeing, and is therefore the authors' accepted manuscript) of the article:
\par
Wood, L. C., Reiners, T., \& Srivastava, H. S. (in press). Think exogenous to excel: Alternative supply chain data to improve transparency and decisions. \emph{International Journal of}
\par
\emph{Logistics: Research and Applications}
\par
contrast, this is unlikely to be useful in business-to-business (B2B) markets or with B2B
\par
products; this is an inherent limitation of the approach.
\par
Within B2C markets, there is a heterogeneity of opinions expressed, with particular product
\par
categories being more commonly commented on than others. When a product is relatively
\par
expensive, consumers are likely to do more on-line research and discussion on the product
\par
before they make a decision to purchase, for example, a large TV or a computer system.
\par
Alternatively, consumers may respond to a mundane product that has experienced change, for
\par
example, a food product with a modified recipe that is perceived as being less palatable. This
\par
occurred when Cadbury's substituted palm oil for traditionally used cocoa butter in their
\par
chocolate (Adams, 2012).
\par
\textbf{\emph{3.3. Discussions can be used to predict retail sales}}
\par
If consumer discussions are uncorrelated with actual sales, it would be pointless to use
\par
sentiment analysis to gauge market demand. However, a growing body of research has
\par
empirically validated that posts, discussions, and comments created by users on social media
\par
sites can be used to predict behaviours of individuals (Goel \& Goldstein, 2013) where such
\par
behaviour may include purchasing decisions.
\par
Empirical evidence also shows that sentiment analysis tools may predict sales volumes.
\par
Therefore, they can be used to create a connection between the release of a product, the overall
\par
online discussion, and then the actual sales of the products. The overall volumes of discussion
\par
can be used to predict or estimate sales volumes (Baek, Ahn, \& Oh, 2014; Duan \& Liao, 2013;
\par
Wang, Cai, \& Huang, 2010). Even the number of fans on Facebook can be correlated with
\par
retail sales (Schultz, 2016). Other researchers note that it is not just the overall volume but also
\par
who the messages are coming from (Lic\u a \& Tu\textcommabelow t\u a, 2011). In this way, ``online [word of mouth]
\par
and expert reviews play a critical role in moviegoers' consumption behavior in the age of the
\par
Internet and social media'' (Kim, Park, \& Park, 2013, p. 98).
\par
While much of the earlier research has focused on predicting sales of box office movie
\par
releases, the same results hold true in other industries. When studying hotel revenue, Blal and
\par
Sturman (2014, p. 373) note that the ``use of [electronic word-of-mouth] data from one time
\par
period to predict [sales performance] in a subsequent time period provides strong support for
\par
the conclusion that [electronic word-of-mouth] indeed has organizational-level effects''. The
\par
use of online product reviews can also improve forecasting accuracy of entertainment goods
\par
(Dellarocas, Zhang, \& Awad, 2007).
\par
In many cases, the value of the sentiment information in reviews lies in supplementing other
\par
forms of analysis, such as with past sales performance (Yu, Liu, Huang, \& An, 2012).
\par
Predictions of box-office revenue can be improved by also using an online prediction game
\par
and an online prediction market (McKenzie, 2013). The use of more data often provides greater
\par
accuracy in forecasts, particularly when using behavioural data from social network services
\par
(SNS) (Kim, Hong, \& Kang, 2015). The use of such `secondary search data' can enable firms
\par
to predict sales better (Chandukala, Dotson, Liu, \& Conrady, 2014).This is the \textbf{post-print} (i.e., it is the authors' final draft, post-refereeing, and is therefore the authors' accepted manuscript) of the article:
