\par
Qin (2011) clearly demonstrated the ability to predict sales in the marketplace, based on
\par
consumer comments on publicly accessible social media. This predictive ability is important
\par
as anyone -- including upstream suppliers -- can access these data (i.e., the consumer
\par
comments), analyse them, and draw the same conclusions. These data are available in real-time
\par
and are simultaneously and equally available to all members of the supply chain. Where the
\par
product in question is a `feature-based' product (e.g., consumer electronics such as a smart
\par
phone), review content will often provide an evaluation of objective features or functions.
\par
Particularly, feature reviews may be valuable when the overall utility of the product is essential
\par
(Cheema \& Papatla, 2010). However, `experience goods' often balance objective evaluation
\par
with a subjective opinion about the experience (Ghose \& Ipeirotis, 2006); here, the
\par
classification and connection may be more closely connected to ``buzz'' about something rather
\par
than objective analysis of it. This nascent area of research requires closer examination of the
\par
moderating role of product category on the nature of reviews and the resulting value of the
\par
reviews within the sentiment analysis process (Maeyer, 2012).
\par
With this empirical validation of the concept, we can assert that the social media postings
\par
may be analysed in terms of product sales for a particular product.
\par
\textbf{\emph{3.4. Automatic analysis and processing of the data must result in summarised information}}
\par
\textbf{\emph{useful for Supply Chain Managers}}
\par
Practically, a manager does not have hours to pore over social media postings nor the ability to
\par
make a gut judgement based on these. Therefore, the data must be able to be analysed and
\par
summarised automatically so that useful information can be included in decision-making
\par
activities. This automatic analysis and summarisation is called `sentiment analysis' or `opinion
\par
mining'.
\par
Most conventional natural language processing (NLP) analyses are based on a combination
\par
of machine learning or `training approaches' (Prabowo \& Thelwall, 2009). In all cases, it is
\par
necessary to understand both words and the concepts behind the words, and how these are
\par
connected to create a useful `parcel' of information. (Cambria and White [2014] provide an
\par
extensive overview of NLP research.)
\par
Just capturing the meaning of the item of text is inadequate -- further details must be
\par
extracted so that the data can be aggregated and analysed to provide useful insight. Chen and
\par
Zimbra (2010) note that a `direct opinion' is a quintuple of five parts of information: the object,
\par
a feature, the orientation of opinion, the opinion holder, and time expressed. Together, these
\par
data enable us to capture what is being said or opined about the product and determine the
\par
strength and direction of that sentiment. In particular, details must be identified about the
\par
object/product being discussed. In many situations, it may be expedient to classify opinions as
\par
`positive', `negative', or `neutral/no opinion', adopting the product classification approach
\par
outlined by Pang and Lee (2008).
\par
When all items have been classified, it is possible to evaluate the total sum of each category
\par
to capture, at a glance, whether the overall opinions are positive or negative. As the overall
\par
stocks change dynamically, these can be obtained and analysed to provide an ``early warning
\par
system'' or a ``canary in the mines'' to feed into proactive decision-making systems.This is the \textbf{post-print} (i.e., it is the authors' final draft, post-refereeing, and is therefore the authors' accepted manuscript) of the article:
