\par
Furthermore, the change in trend or direction of sentiment can be detected and determined with
\par
automatic processing/algorithms (Kim \& Kim, 2014).
\par
\textbf{4. Theoretical framework and propositions}
\par
A framework for the incorporation of sentiment analysis into firms' decision-making to
\par
improve supply chain performance is proposed (Figure 3). Also, support from the theory of the
\par
BWE and strategic fit is provided for stronger theoretical support where appropriate.
\par
\textbf{Figure 3. The Sentiment-Analysis driven supply chain process framework}
\par
\textbf{\emph{4.1 Reflection and design phase}}
\par
As highlighted by Dooley et al. (2010), having information does not ensure that it is used;
\par
managerially focused research must support decision-makers using the information provided
\par
through IS. Such an incorporation of multiple sources of data is not necessarily achieved
\par
instantly; the problem is not simply to provide data but also to ensure effective integration into
\par
decision-making processes, indicating the importance of psychological principles. Therefore,
\par
the first phase is based on \emph{dual-process theory}, which assumes that autonomous or intuitive
\par
mental processing takes place unless there is intervention involving a higher-order reasoning
\par
process (Barr, Pennycook, Stolz, \& Fugelsang, 2015; Evans \& Stanovich, 2013).
\par
Frederick (2005, p. 35) proposes that an individual's \emph{cognitive reflection} is the ``ability or
\par
disposition to resist reporting the response that first comes to mind''
\par
. In our context, it refers to
\par
a manager using the best sources or making a carefully weighed judgement, rather than only
\par
relying on the most pressing source of information. A fast or autonomous judgement about
\par
information would lead managers to rely on existing forecasts less critically; this represents a
\par
Type 1 process (Evans \& Stanovich, 2013). In the case reported by Dooley et al. (2010), weakThis is the \textbf{post-print} (i.e., it is the authors' final draft, post-refereeing, and is therefore the authors' accepted manuscript) of the article:
\par
Wood, L. C., Reiners, T., \& Srivastava, H. S. (in press). Think exogenous to excel: Alternative supply chain data to improve transparency and decisions. \emph{International Journal of}
\par
\emph{Logistics: Research and Applications}
\par
cognitive reflection might lead to managers basing their decisions only on the retailer orders,
\par
rather than also making use of the retailer demand data. Individuals with a developed cognitive
\par
reflection would be more able to assess the complete range of information sources -- including
\par
those derived from the sentiment analysis approach we have outlined -- and build these into a
\par
more comprehensive set of supply chain plans for the organisation. This `cognitive reflection'
\par
on the data may be a valuable skill for many managers. We might infer that as we saw in Dooley
\par
et al. (2010), the managers, concerned with the more immediately pressing KPIs relating to
\par
their immediate downstream customers, rather than the overall health of the supply chain,
\par
might be those with lower levels of cognitive reflection. Rather, managers need to be concerned
\par
with the environmental changes and take the time to incorporate these factors in decisions, if
\par
they wish optimal outcomes (Leaptrott \& McDonald, 2015).
\par
Managers possessing high cognitive reflection should be able to integrate a range of data.
\par
This is alluded to by Dooley et al. (2010, p. 17) when they note that firms ``must balance local
\par
and global information, and the recent emphasis on integrated supply chains, which tends to
\par
focus on immediate upstream and downstream coupling, may have led to a decision
\par
environment where `external' news was relatively ignored''. These external and in many cases
\par
more ambiguous factors will have greater influence if managers have higher levels of cognitive
\par
reflection and the ability and willing to engage in Type 2 processes (Barr et al., 2015). Type 2
\par
processes are higher-order, careful, and reasoned judgements that incorporate these exogenous
\par
factors into the decision-making processes. Sentiment analysis may not provide exact or
\par
precisely quantifiable movement or shifts of demand that other approaches might. Rather,
\par
sentiment analysis would be one of a range of input information into supply chain decisions.
\par
Therefore, sentiment analysis can provide early access to insight into market-based demand
\par
data but will only deliver value when managers carefully use this. Therefore, managers with
\par
higher levels of cognitive reflection are better-positioned to use sentiment analysis in supply
\par
chain decisions. We therefore assert that managers possessing higher levels of cognitive
\par
reflection will be more successful in combining a range of data and, therefore:
\par
\emph{H1: Exogenous factors will have more influence on decisions in users with high levels}
\par
\emph{of cognitive reflection.}
\par
``[I]ndividuals high in need for cognition are thought to be more likely to expend effort on
\par
information acquisition, reasoning, and problem solving to cope with a wide variety of
\par
predicaments in their world'' (Cacioppo, Petty, Feinstein, \& Jarvis, 1996, p. 199).
\par
Almost axiomatically, having last-minute access to information requires the use of existing
\par
decision-making frameworks. A lack of time removes the opportunity to reflect, resulting in
\par
managers' `expediting' decisions with short timeframes (Leaptrott \& McDonald, 2008).
\par
Therefore:
\par
\emph{H2: Early access to information from exogenous factors will have more influence on}
\par
\emph{decisions in users with high levels of cognitive reflection.}This is the \textbf{post-print} (i.e., it is the authors' final draft, post-refereeing, and is therefore the authors' accepted manuscript) of the article:
