The premise that effective design leads to improved execution has been examined in
\par
operational contexts: careful design of product-process design to improve new-product
\par
development (NPD) processes (Lu \& Wood, 2006); effective design of customer-experience
\par
systems to promote loyalty (Fawcett, Fawcett, Cooper, \& Daynes, 2014); or the use of sales
\par
and operations planning (S\&OP) as a `decision-making forum' for both tactical and strategic
\par
decisions (Stank, Dittmann, \& Autry, 2011).
\par
Therefore, it is proposed that:
\par
\emph{H3: The reflection and design phase will lead to the execution phase}
\par
\textbf{\emph{4.2 Execution and decisions}}
\par
Following the reflection and design of mechanisms to include and use sentiment analysis, the
\par
planning and execution phase will take place. Based on the theory of the BWE, we know that
\par
having earlier access to marketplace information enables firms to analyse and compare actual
\par
order information with demand signals obtained from sentiment analysis. This helps firms to
\par
counteract behavioural causes of the BWE and support them to develop and execute plans to
\par
better position themselves for changes in the marketplace. The additional information from
\par
sentiment analysis contributes to firms' ``need to coordinate activities, integrate operations, and
\par
balance supply/demand across supply chain members'' (Thomas, Defee, Randall, \& Williams,
\par
2011, p. 664), as influenced internally by the S\&OP process.
\par
Amongst the sectors examined by Dooley et al. (2010), the automotive supply chains stood
\par
alone insofar as upstream firms in this sector did not experience an increased volatility of
\par
demand or orders being placed. These automotive supply chains are more likely to be shorter
\par
than other supply chains (note that the use of aggregated data by Dooley et al. [2010] limits our
\par
ability to specify this). However, the lack of forecasting and resulting demand-supply
\par
mismatches are an area of concern in agri-fresh produce supply chains (Shukla \& Jharkharia,
\par
2013), which are also often short chains.
\par
In traditional S\&OP processes, the effort to balance supply and demand may rely on shared
\par
forecasts from customers or forward orders. Complex environments benefit from the use of
\par
more information in the analysis (e.g., through APS) to support S\&OP (Kjellsdotter Ivert \&
\par
Jonsson, 2014). In the future, incorporation of sentiment analysis might enable the `demand-
\par
side' meetings to consider the use of sentiment analysis to derive marketplace changes where
\par
this factor would be an influence in the decision-making process. ``The S\&OP process is part
\par
of the operational and line management processes. It is through this process that the operational
\par
alignment to strategy can be managed and implemented'' (Godsell, Birtwistle, \& Hoek, 2010,
\par
p. 14). By including sentiment analysis as an augmentation of the standardised S\&OP process,
\par
the execution phases can be enhanced, by improving some of the underlying behavioural
\par
comprehension of time delays, feedback, and other behavioural challenges to effective supply
\par
management.
\par
While sentiment analysis holds the promise of quickly and effectively allowing an
\par
assessment of market-based demand, we certainly do not claim that this is, by itself, a ``silver-This is the \textbf{post-print} (i.e., it is the authors' final draft, post-refereeing, and is therefore the authors' accepted manuscript) of the article:
