\par
bullet'' or a panacea to some of the difficulties that we have outlined. Rather, we envisage the
\par
value from our proposed approach as an early warning system and a source of information that
\par
is not necessarily correlated with other (existing) sources; it acts as a support to counteract
\par
behavioural issues that relate to poor decision-making. This alternate perspective may be
\par
important as it can show a divergence in what is occurring in the environment, and is able to
\par
give pause for thought to managers possessing high levels of cognitive reflection. Mature and
\par
effective S\&OP processes and leadership of these processes enable effective S\&OP integration
\par
and effectiveness (Grimson \& Pyke, 2007), indicating a high level of initial involvement.
\par
Sentiment analysis would, therefore, be a single influence and source of data amongst several
\par
others.
\par
Sentiment analysis can inform decisions taken ahead of time. When information is received
\par
earlier, it can feed into the S\&OP process, enabling a better balance or matching between the
\par
ability for the firm to produce or supply the product in the future vs. what the anticipated
\par
demand will be. These processes include an analysis of required supply, consisting of
\par
production or inventory volume and capacity, to meet the anticipated demand. Understanding
\par
demand earlier will enable further changes to supply-side decisions (e.g., more time to develop
\par
a larger inventory stock in anticipation of an increase in orders coming through the supply
\par
chain). ``A well-supported sales and operations planning and rough-cut planning of production
\par
quantities ensures production at the lowest [total cost of ownership], with respect to
\par
manufacturing productivity, material and logistic costs'' (Fuchs \& Otto, 2015, p. 88).
\par
Therefore, the earlier inclusion of sentiment analysis in the analysis enables more adjustment
\par
to lower these costs.
\par
Where there are significant changes in market requirements, the higher market volatility and
\par
uncertainty would suggest that early access to data regarding what is happening in the market
\par
will be most valuable. Managers require an appropriate infrastructure with integrated systems
\par
and technologies to benefit from the information and react accordingly.
\par
From these discussions, it becomes clear that firms have recognised the importance of
\par
market changes for decision-making. Management techniques and technology solutions enable
\par
faster oversight of market changes, remove the `barrier' that firms between the focal firm and
\par
the market represent, and effectively position the firm so it is `chronologically closer' to what
\par
is occurring in the market. At the foundation of this effort is the awareness that knowing market
\par
changes early on enables firms to make better decisions and undertake better-planned actions.
\par
Earlier access to this market-demand data will have a greater impact on operational plans
\par
involving production or inventory volume and capacity.
\par
Therefore, we propose:
\par
\emph{H4: Early access to data using sentiment analysis will have a greater impact on}
\par
\emph{adjustments of operational plans involving volumes and capacity}
\par
The use of market-based data is important, as it allows firms to avoid the information
\par
distortion commonly associated with the BWE (Chen \& Lee, 2012). Improved access to
\par
market-based data is most useful where it is incorporated into efficient decision-making.This is the \textbf{post-print} (i.e., it is the authors' final draft, post-refereeing, and is therefore the authors' accepted manuscript) of the article:
