\par
Following the Global Financial Crisis (GFC) from 2007 to 2009, Dooley et al. (2010) examined
\par
the amplification of demand and transference of order variability over supply chains during
\par
this tumultuous period. The research was based in the US and encompassed retailers,
\par
wholesalers, and manufacturers. We can infer that these were (relative to the rest of the world)
\par
well-resourced firms, acting in one of the largest and most sophisticated markets in the world.
\par
If there were management techniques or technologies that could improve supply chain
\par
management, these are the firms that should have been employing them. As a result, we would
\par
have expected these firms to have managed successfully throughout the Global Finance Crisis
\par
and that this should have been reflected in their management in the face of changes in market
\par
demand and order variability. However, despite their significant advantages, Dooley et al.
\par
(2010, pp. 16-17; emphasis added) noted that ``[w]holesalers may have been paying too much
\par
attention to retailer demand, and not enough attention to\textbf{ }consumer demand, \emph{even though such}
\par
\emph{data was readily available to them}''
\par
. This insight indicates that the proximity to the end
\par
consumer \emph{should} have allowed the firms to understand the shift in demand and then respond to
\par
it, in near-real-time -- but they failed to do so; instead, behavioural problems contributed to
\par
poor decision-making.
\par
As expected, the classical outcome occurred whereby the BWE most impacted firms furthest
\par
from the market. Dooley et al. (2010, p. 15) noted that ``[b]ecause of [the wholesalers'] farther
\par
distance from the consumer, they were slower to react, and this led to the need for them to take
\par
more drastic action''. During the recession, market demand and retail sales shifted, significantly
\par
increasing the standard deviation of orders upstream. The uncertainty, exacerbated by the
\par
BWE, affected firms distant from the market who were not attending to market-based data.
\par
Alignment of activities and processes over the supply chain often occurs to reduce the
\par
impact on the BWE on suppliers. When these projects take place, there is ``improved alignment
\par
[as the] supply base becomes more agile and able to respond rapidly to customer requirements
\par
due to improved understanding of these requirements'' (Handfield, Cousins, Lawson, \&
\par
Petersen, 2015, pp. 13-14). Particular technology-driven planning improvements, such as the
\par
use of ERP, often lead to improved supply management (Huang \& Handfield, 2015, p. 16).
\par
However, a range of planning and execution activities may be required as combinations of
\par
activities often create synergistic benefits to the firm (Handfield et al., 2015). Synergistic
\par
benefits can accrue particularly where the challenges in improving planning processes come
\par
not from technical elements but behavioural causes of poor information sharing and use, driven
\par
by individuals in the system (Wallace \& Stahl, 2006). Therefore, the ability to plan and execute
\par
based on market-demand, rather than the market demand itself, can generate positive supply
\par
chain outcomes.
\par
Early understanding of changes in the market enables a firm to plan their supply
\par
management activities more appropriately. Effective supply management planning supports
\par
positive financial and operational outcomes for firms. Therefore, we present the following
\par
hypothesis:
\par
\emph{H5: Executed plans based on sentiment analysis will have an influence on supply chain}
\par
\emph{outcomes.}This is the \textbf{post-print} (i.e., it is the authors' final draft, post-refereeing, and is therefore the authors' accepted manuscript) of the article:
