\textbf{\emph{4.3 Supply chain outcomes}}
\par
Supply chain outcomes can be improved by counteracting the BWE. Therefore, early access to
\par
and effective use of information can allow operational decisions to be made that minimise costs
\par
of the firm and position them to respond more quickly to market changes.
\par
Implicit in the theory of the BWE is that the amplification of demand, and the poor outcomes
\par
that many actors create, reduce their ability to respond appropriately. This is important as
\par
``[d]emand response is the ability to anticipate or handle changes in marketplace demand''
\par
(Ralston, Blackhurst, Cantor, \& Crum, 2015, p. 48) and firms' demand response and integration
\par
within the firm has an impact on cycle time process performance and financial performance
\par
(Ralston et al., 2015).
\par
A firm can become increasingly distant from the market that their output eventually serves,
\par
by the increasing levels of outsourcing along the chain. Where suppliers are further upstream
\par
(i.e., there are many tiers between themselves and the marketplace of final consumers), access
\par
to marketplace demand data becomes more useful, and performance improves. In shorter
\par
supply chains, manufacturers can evaluate market demand changes; for example, a food
\par
manufacturer often occupies a short chain and is often domestic, providing closer access to
\par
market data and managers will have some awareness of market requirements. ``[S]upply chain
\par
performance depends on effectively integrating supply and demand'' (Thomas et al., 2011, p.
\par
662) as the integration allows the firm to create an appropriate demand response. The further
\par
the firm is from the market, the more time the firm will have to create an appropriate response.
\par
Long chains are also logically correlated with slower information transfer along the chain.
\par
Therefore, a lengthy chain where the focal firm is far upstream from the market will present
\par
the opportunity for a supplier to benefit more from early access to market-focused data. The
\par
supplier can then position themselves, compete for scarce suppliers or customers, and organise
\par
their production and inventory schedules ahead of time to prepare better for the coming
\par
increase/decrease in consumer orders. Earlier access to information means more time to make
\par
effective planning decisions and execute optimally. Improved planning should lead to lower
\par
inventories to meet required fill rates (providing cost benefits) and also enable the firm to more
\par
quickly meet customer orders (improving time-to-market). Such an impact can be seen, albeit
\par
over few echelons and based on forecasts rather than market demand, where order variability
\par
decreases (Lu, Tsai, Chen, \& Lee, 2013). Reduction in order variability is a common result,
\par
where customer information from several downstream echelons can be used to improve the
\par
supply chain performance at a given firm (Kumar, Mukherjee, \& Kumar, 2013)
\par
Understanding changes in markets may be easily achieved with short, transparent, and
\par
tightly integrated supply chains (e.g., a virtually vertically integrated supply chain). However,
\par
most managers do not have the luxury of selecting the attributes that they want; instead, they
\par
face these attributes as a given in their particular type of supply chain facing exogenous
\par
variables and are therefore constrained and unable to change these attributes. The possibility
\par
remains for a firm to look downstream, towards the marketplace, and use sentiment analysis to
\par
gauge whether consumer demand is changing.This is the \textbf{post-print} (i.e., it is the authors' final draft, post-refereeing, and is therefore the authors' accepted manuscript) of the article:
