\par
Firms distant from the final consumers of their product will benefit more from
\par
understanding the changes in marketplace demand. Sentiment analysis can provide them with
\par
a better understanding of significant changes, and it can do this in real-time, faster than they
\par
could gain similar insight through collaborative or information-sharing approaches along the
\par
supply chain. The general principle should apply, that the further upstream a firm is, the more
\par
valuable the market demand data is, and therefore we hypothesise that:
\par
\emph{H6: Execution based on sentiment analysis data will have a stronger effect on supply}
\par
\emph{chain performance where the focal firm is distant from the market.}
\par
Improved supply chain performance provides a greater likelihood for greater use of data on
\par
external influences. First, firms already capture manufacturing planning data (e.g., product
\par
breakdown and relationships in BOM files, and inventory levels) that are internal. Second,
\par
firms already engage in forecasting processes and are able to make the appropriate connections
\par
between the outcome of the internal proccesses into other decision-making processes (e.g.,
\par
through the use of S\&OP). Therefore, a method is needed to integrate changes in sentiment
\par
analysis outcomes with existing forecasts. Firms that have seen and evaluated benefits from a
\par
limited pilot will be more willing to invest further resources in developing these capabilities
\par
further. This method, of combining the insights with traditional/existing forecasting, is a task
\par
we leave to future researchers, but we have presented a model to demonstrate where the
\par
analytical results `fit' into the existing information systems architecture. This type of success
\par
in a method often sees a further uptake in the future, for example, as observed in the use of
\par
QSAM methodology by Atilgan and McCullen (2011). Such models often focus on an iterative
\par
cycle of improvement and support effective executions with a focus on continual
\par
improvements. Therefore, we propose:
\par
\emph{H7: Improved supply chain outcomes improves ability to access exogenous factors and}
\par
\emph{use these in decision-making.}
\par
\textbf{5. Theoretical and managerial implications and limitations}
\par
In this exploratory and conceptual paper, we have highlighted the existing difficulties and
\par
challenges associated with accessing market-place (i.e., consumer demand) information when
\par
a given firm is upstream from the market. Incorporating the market-place information into
\par
decisions should lead to improved supply chain performance. The improved performance
\par
comes from the way that sentiment analysis can be used to counteract behavioural elements
\par
contributing to the BWE. A framework is presented based on literature and the dual-process
\par
theory and theory of the BWE; however, this framework has not been empirically validated
\par
yet. Thus, while our propositions are theoretically grounded and are conceptually appealing,
\par
they can probably be further refined through analysis of the phenomena in practice. Given the
\par
acceptance of the underlying frameworks, several theoretical and managerial implications can
\par
be inferred.This is the \textbf{post-print} (i.e., it is the authors' final draft, post-refereeing, and is therefore the authors' accepted manuscript) of the article:
\par
Wood, L. C., Reiners, T., \& Srivastava, H. S. (in press). Think exogenous to excel: Alternative supply chain data to improve transparency and decisions. \emph{International Journal of}
\par
\emph{Logistics: Research and Applications}
\par
What has to change to counteract the BWE effectively? Information is crucial and, therefore,
\par
access to direct market data is a logical addition to information passed upstream, that is, as a
\par
supplement to orders from customers. The use of sentiment analysis -- as an alternative and
\par
emerging approach to gain direct insight on marketplaces -- has been introduced in this paper
\par
as a technology. However, we note that this method is not a silver bullet and suffers weaknesses
\par
in the practical application. Sentiment analysis should, therefore, only be used as part of a wider
\par
portfolio of approaches when making supply chain decisions as it can contribute to reducing
\par
behavioural impediments to effective decision-making but may not be valuable for precise
\par
forecasts in the execution phases of supply management.
\par
By positioning sentiment analysis as a tool with latent value to supply chain managers, we
\par
hope to provoke greater interest in these emerging analytic approaches and how they can be
\par
incorporated into existing analysis methods.
\par
Even though firms have undertaken considerable work to improve IT investments and
\par
dyadic information flow, there is still evidence that existing approaches to combatting the BWE
\par
are not entirely effective. First, they do not include recognition of dual-process theory and the
\par
difficulty in incorporating and using information, particularly that which is longer-term and
\par
less reliable. From our first phase, we can see that if there are difficulties incorporating this
\par
information, it will have little impact on downstream processes (i.e., those downstream in the
\par
decision-making process). Second, existing dyadic information-sharing processes do not
\par
explicitly require inclusion in existing planning and execution processes. Our model addresses
\par
this through connecting with the commonly used S\&OP processes and reducing behavioural
\par
challenges to improved supply management. Third, we focus on a continual improvement
\par
cycle, where benefits from one cycle should lead to greater examination and use of exogenous
\par
factors to further improve planning, rather than one-off processes. This dynamic improvement
\par
cycle requires sustained attention and effort.
\par
Even though the proposed framework consists of a series of qualitative propositions that are
\par
refutable, there is still the challenge to conduct an in-depth case study to test or further refine
\par
the propositions. This case study would uncover elements that firms are using to improve or
\par
aid the use of sentiment analysis in their supply chain decision-making processes, and these
\par
elements may be used to enhance the model.This is the \textbf{post-print} (i.e., it is the authors' final draft, post-refereeing, and is therefore the authors' accepted manuscript) of the article:
