\par
DOI 10.1007/s10708-016-9699-x
\par
Utilizing fuzzy set theory to assure the quality of volunteered
\par
geographic information
\par
Yingwei Yan. Chen-Chieh Feng. Yi-Chen Wang
\par
Published online: 19 February 2016
\par
\'O Springer Science+Business Media Dordrecht 2016
\par
Abstract This paper presents a fuzzy system to
\par
assure the quality of volunteered geographic informa-
\par
tion (VGI) collected for the purposes of species
\par
surveillances. The system uses trust as a proxy of
\par
quality. It defines the trust using both the provenance
\par
of user expertise and the fitness of geographic context
\par
and quantifies it using fuzzy set theory. The system
\par
was applied to a specific scenario---VGI-based crop
\par
pest surveillance---to demonstrate its usefulness in
\par
handling VGI quality. A case study was conducted in
\par
Jiangxi province of China, where location-based rice
\par
pest surveillance reports generated by the local
\par
farmers were collected. A field pest survey was
\par
conducted by the local pest management experts to
\par
verify the farmer-generated reports, and the survey
\par
results were used as ground truth data. The quality of
\par
the farmer-generated reports were also assessed
\par
through the fuzzy system and compared to the pest
\par
survey results. It was observed that the degree to which
\par
these two sets of results agreed to each other was
\par
satisfactory.
\par
Keywords Volunteered geographic information
\par
Data quality Fuzzy system Species surveillance
\par
Y. Yan (\&) C.-C. Feng Y.-C. Wang
\par
Department of Geography, 1 Arts Link, National
\par
University of Singapore, Singapore 117570, Singapore
\par
e-mail: yanyingwei@u.nus.edu
\par
C.-C. Feng
\par
e-mail: geofcc@nus.edu.sg
\par
Y.-C. Wang
\par
e-mail: geowyc@nus.edu.sg
\par
Introduction
\par
The increasing use of volunteered geographic infor-
\par
mation (VGI) across various application domains has
\par
revolutionized how spatial knowledge can be derived.
\par
This is attributed to many of the advantages of VGI,
\par
notably the ability to provide pervasive location-based
\par
data and to allow more flexible data collection
\par
mechanisms. These features not only enable better
\par
reflections of the observations of ubiquitous users on
\par
the ground, but also facilitate the capture of data that
\par
may be otherwise left out by traditional data collection
\par
means.
\par
Among the applications characterized by their
\par
increasing reliance on VGI efforts, species surveil-
\par
lances have attracted considerable attention from
\par
researchers (Goodchild 2007; Zhu et al. 2015). The
\par
use of high quality VGI for these purposes has
\par
implications for scientific inquiries pertaining to
\par
environmental, economic, health security, and by
\par
extension, sustainability issues.
\par
VGI systems for such purposes can be readily built
\par
using existing off-the-shelf computing technologies.
\par
However, it remains challenging to assure the quality
\par
of VGI so as to derive valuable knowledge. Indeed,
\par
any applications involving VGI inevitably face the
\par
123518 GeoJournal (2017) 82:517--532
\par
challenges of high diversity and a greater level of data
\par
uncertainty (Goodchild and Li 2012; Kuhn 2007)
\par
because VGI can be developed by both authoritative
\par
agencies and amateur communities (Foody et al. 2013)
\par
as well as contributors of varying levels of knowledge
\par
and expertise (Goodchild 2009; Tulloch 2008). Fur-
\par
thermore, VGI can be created for various personal
\par
purposes (Coleman et al. 2009) and collected without
\par
explicit quality control measures (Girres and Touya
\par
2010) or metadata (Brando and Bucher 2010). Ensur-
\par
ing meaningful gathering of intelligence from VGI
\par
thus demands careful treatments with reference to
\par
such data. In other words, it is crucial to measure and
\par
ensure that VGI is of high degree of data quality.
\par
Therefore, this paper proposes a system to assure the
\par
quality of VGI collected for the purposes of species
\par
surveillances. The system takes advantage of fuzzy set
\par
theory to handle data uncertainty and ambiguity
\par
inherent in VGI contributions, incorporating explicitly
\par
the unique property of VGI--trust. To demonstrate the
\par
usefulness of the fuzzy system in handling VGI
\par
quality, it was applied to a specific case scenario, i.e., a
\par
VGI-based crop pest surveillance.
\par
The following section reviews how the quality of
\par
VGI is assured by the approaches from existing work.
\par
It also illustrates the related shortcomings. To improve
\par
the existing approaches of VGI quality assurance, a
\par
fuzzy system is then presented. It is followed by a
\par
discussion on the features of the fuzzy system and the
\par
future directions of this line of research. The last
\par
section concludes the paper.
\par
Assuring the quality of VGI
\par
Direct and indirect approaches
\par
Several existing studies on VGI quality assurance have
\par
adopted a direct approach which compares a VGI
\par
dataset to an authoritative gold standard reference
\par
dataset. For example, Zielstra and Zipf (2010) examined
\par
the completeness of a German OpenStreetMap dataset
\par
in comparison to a TeleAtlas MultiNet dataset. Haklay
\par
(2010) compared a London OpenStreetMap dataset with
\par
an Ordnance Survey dataset based on positional accu-
\par
racy and completeness. More comprehensively, Girres
\par
and Touya (2010) extended the work of Haklay (2010)
\par
by comparing a French OpenStreeMap dataset with a
\par
BD TOPO\`O IGN dataset based on a larger set of spatial
\par
data quality elements.
\par
Such a direct approach can be seen as an adoption
\par
of the traditional data quality assessment method that
\par
focuses on internal data quality (Devillers et al. 2005).
\par
It, however, has limited applicability for assuring the
\par
quality of VGI as there is generally an absence of
\par
authoritative gold standard reference datasets for VGI
\par
applications (Bishr 2007; Kuhn 2007). For example, in
\par
the case of utilizing VGI for species surveillances,
\par
voluntary observations are often conducted in sparsely
\par
populated, rural, or less explored areas of the world. In
\par
such a case the gold standard reference datasets are
\par
often lacking. In addition, VGI dataset is often more
\par
up-to-date than authoritative dataset and thus may be
\par
more accurate than the so called gold standard
\par
reference dataset (Goodchild and Li 2012). To cope
\par
with this issue, indirect approaches relying on surro-
\par
gate criteria were proposed. Four mainstream indirect
\par
approaches are described as follows:
\par
1. The user review approach (Goodchild and Li
\par
2012; Maue \textasciiacute{} and Schade 2008). This approach is
\par
user-driven and relies on Linus' Law which
\par
assumes that ``given enough eyes all bugs are
\par
shallow''. Based on Linus' Law, user contribu-
\par
tions converge on a truth through an iterative error
\par
correction process, either in terms of attributive
\par
error or positional error, or both. If one user
\par
commits an error, the error can be detected or
\par
corrected by the other users. Haklay et al. (2010)
\par
have applied this approach to OpenStreetMap and
\par
suggested its applicability to VGI in general.
\par
2. The provenance approach (Celino 2013; Trame
\par
and Ke\ss ler 2011). This approach relies on the
\par
history of volunteered information. Requesting or
\par
tracing the history of a VGI dataset (e.g., who are
\par
the data providers?) is helpful in better under-
\par
standing and assessing its quality.
\par
3. The geographic approach (Goodchild and Li
\par
2012). This approach is based on Tobler's first
\par
law of geography, which assumes things that are
\par
closer are more related than things that are farther
\par
apart (Tobler 1970). A VGI contribution should fit
\par
its geographic context, e.g., a report of a species
\par
occurrence is more likely to be true if many
\par
similar reports exist nearby. In addition, more
\par
credit can be given to a VGI if it is volunteered by
\par
a local resident who is physically close to the site
\par
123GeoJournal (2017) 82:517--532 519
\par
4. of the VGI event and is familiar with the local
\par
environment (Seeger 2008).
\par
The trust approach (Bishr 2007; Bishr and Janow-
\par
icz 2010; Bishr and Mantelas 2008). It uses trust as
\par
a proxy of quality to establish a link between VGI
\par
quality and VGI contributors' authority based on
\par
subjective evaluations. It rests on the extent to
\par
which a VGI contributor has provided honest and
\par
accurate information. Trusted VGI contributors
\par
tend to provide more trustworthy information
\par
compared to less trusted ones. The criteria for
\par
evaluating the trustworthiness of VGI replace
\par
traditional quality measures of geospatial infor-
\par
mation (e.g., completeness, logical consistency,
\par
and positional accuracy). Indeed, the information
\par
asymmetry and imperfection of a VGI environ-
\par
ment can lead to social uncertainties in VGI
\par
consumptions (Sniezek and Van Swol 2001).
\par
When high social uncertainties exist, trust appears
\par
to be particularly important as it reduces social
\par
uncertainties by confining the range of behavior
\par
expected from another (Sniezek and Van Swol
\par
2001).
\par
Challenges in using the indirect approaches
\par
for species surveillance applications
\par
Among the indirect approaches, the user review
\par
approach works well for those VGI that are more
\par
traceable, such as those in Wikimapia and Open-
\par
StreetMap. However, it is problematic for species
\par
surveillance applications because the objects being
\par
recorded are often highly mobile or persist for only a
\par
short period of time. It is hardly possible to go back to
\par
the reported locations to verify every user surveillance
\par
report and therefore it is not peer-reviewable. Good-
\par
child and Li (2012) also pointed out that this approach
\par
works less well for obscure phenomena, including
\par
those short-lived ones. Conducting the review process
\par
for time-critical issues (e.g., pest outbreaks) is also
\par
impossible because the process is generally time-
\par
consuming. Additionally, Linus' Law sometimes fails.
\par
In a crowdsourcing-based cropland capture game,
\par
Salk et al. (2015) demonstrated that the majority
\par
agreement among volunteers cannot fully substitute
\par
the quality assessment by experts on crowdsourced
\par
tasks.
\par
The provenance approach, geographic approach,
\par
and trust approach appear to be applicable for species
\par
surveillance applications. However, when used alone,
\par
all three approaches fall short in fully describing VGI
\par
data quality.
\par
The provenance approach considers VGI prove-
\par
nance, including data contributors' expertise. What is
\par
challenging, though, is how to appropriately incorpo-
\par
rate provenance of user expertise as the expertise level
\par
of a VGI contributor is difficult to collect (Ke\ss ler et al.
\par
2009). There are also resistances in providing such
\par
information due to the concerns on personal privacy
\par
and security (Song and Sun 2010). According to
\par
Coleman et al. (2009), VGI contributors can be
\par
classified into five types: (1) neophyte, (2) interested
\par
amateur, (3) expert amateur, (4) expert professional,
\par
and (5) expert authority. Normally, people are inclined
\par
to trust contributors who are expert professional and
\par
expert authoritative. However, a contributor considered
\par
to be an expert may understand a project's specification
\par
very well but lack the knowledge of local history or
\par
attributes. A contributor considered as either a neophyte
\par
or interested amateur may know little about the
\par
professional part of a VGI project but is very familiar
\par
with the characteristics and details of his or her current
\par
location. In short, the boundary between non-expert
\par
amateur and expert professional is quickly blurring in
\par
VGI environments where the expertise of a contributor
\par
cannot be simply judged based on contributor type.
\par
As for the geographic approach, considering only
\par
fitness of geographic context tends to be less effective
\par
if a user report fits surrounding geographic context
\par
well but actually is a false observation.
\par
Regarding the trust approach, how trust as a proxy
\par
of quality can be effectively realized in VGI contexts
\par
is problematic. It demands appropriate methods to
\par
evaluate and quantify the trustworthiness of VGI. In
\par
Bishr and Mantelas (2008) an approach combining the
\par
trust approach and the geographic approach was
\par
proposed to assure VGI quality. Their work does
\par
provide valuable insights into the usage of the proxy.
\par
First, indeed, the four indirect approaches reviewed
\par
here are not mutually exclusive. For instance, some of
\par
the elements of trust fall under the geographic
\par
approach, i.e., the trustworthiness of VGI can be
\par
assessed based on geographic contexts. Second, their
\par
approach leverages crowd's dual roles in VGI
\par
123520 GeoJournal (2017) 82:517--532
\par
creation---contributing locational data and ascertain-
\par
ing the reliability of data (i.e., user trust rating). The
\par
second role can be helpful in evaluating the trustwor-
\par
thiness of VGI. Despite these insights, in the combined
\par
approach, fuzziness that is inherent in trust (Chang
\par
et al. 2005) is not well accounted for. Assessing the
\par
quality of VGI based on trust requires special atten-
\par
tions to the fuzzy nature of trust.
\par
Novel approaches thus are called for to synthesize
\par
the advantages and minimize the disadvantages of the
\par
approaches mentioned above to assure the quality of
\par
VGI, with a better way to account for user expertise,
\par
geographic context, and fuzziness involved in trust
\par
judgment.
\par
quality and the nature of trust being inherently fuzzy
\par
(Chang et al. 2005), adopting fuzzy set theory to assess
\par
the quality of VGI is likely to capture more accurately
\par
the whole assessment process.
\par
A fuzzy system
\par
To address the problems mentioned above, we present
\par
a rule-based fuzzy system to assure the quality of user-
\par
generated species surveillance reports. The system
\par
uses trust as a proxy of quality, considering both the
\par
track record of the VGI contributors (i.e., provenance
\par
of user expertise) and the fitness of geographic context
\par
as defining factors of the trust.
\par
Fuzziness in geospatial data quality
\par
Traditionally, geospatial data quality is categorized
\par
into internal quality or external quality (Devillers et al.
\par
2005). The former refers to the assessment of the
\par
difference between a dataset and the reality it repre-
\par
sents. The latter refers to the fitness for use, the extent
\par
to which a dataset can be a good fit for its different
\par
uses. Evaluating geospatial data quality of both kinds
\par
involves using realities or fitness as the baseline for
\par
comparison. The result of the comparison is clear-cut,
\par
or ``crisp'', as they can either be meeting or failing to
\par
meet the standard.
\par
From a user perspective, VGI quality may be
\par
considered by users to be meeting the standard or
\par
slightly below standard, implying a transition between
\par
all levels of quality. It is extremely limiting to treat a
\par
VGI that is slightly below the standard in the same way
\par
as another VGI that virtually fails to meet the standard.
\par
Yongting (1996) proposed the concept of fuzzy quality
\par
to account for such a transition by expressing the
\par
quality with a fuzzy set instead of Boolean logic. In
\par
addition, given the role of trust in evaluating VGI data
\par
123
\par
Fuzzy set theory
\par
Fuzzy set was first introduced by Zadeh (1965) to
\par
model continuous phenomena. It generalizes conven-
\par
tional crisp sets by allowing their elements to have
\par
degrees of membership. The membership is defined by
\par
mapping every element x from a universe of discourse
\par
X to an interval [0, 1], representing the degree to which
\par
x is an element of a fuzzy set, expressed as Eq. 1.
\par
lA\dh x\TH{} : X ! {$\frac{1}{2}$}0; 1 ; where
\par
lA\dh x\TH{} {$\frac{1}{4}$} 1 if x is totally in the fuzzy set;
\par
lA\dh x\TH{} {$\frac{1}{4}$} 0 if x is not in the fuzzy set;
\par
0\textbackslash lA\dh x\TH\textbackslash 1 if x is partly in the fuzzy set:
\par
\dh 1\TH{}
\par
Fuzzy set is often used for modelling subjective
\par
human reasoning using natural languages in which
\par
many expressions have vague or imprecise meanings
\par
(Caha et al. 2012). It is therefore a prominent
\par
alternative to more traditional modelling paradigms
\par
for addressing complex, ill-defined, and less
\par
tractable systems (Manca and Curtin 2012). In geog-
\par
raphy, fuzzy set has been applied to modelling the
\par
uncertainty inherent in spatial datasets (Al-kheder
\par
et al. 2008; Zhang et al. 2014).
\par
System development
\par
To introduce the system development based on fuzzy
\par
set theory, the following sections first describe its core
\par
fuzzy inference method. Then the two input variables
\par
(i.e., provenance of user expertise and fitness of
\par
geographic context), one output variable (i.e., the
\par
trustworthiness of user reports), and fuzzy rules of the
\par
system are defined. Last, the system usage is
\par
introduced.
\par
Fuzzy inference
\par
Mamdani-style fuzzy inference is adopted in the
\par
system as it is better suited to handling fuzziness and
\par
data uncertainty and it works better with human inputs
\par
(Power et al. 2001). The inference requires the
\par
developer to create both input and output membershipGeoJournal (2017) 82:517--532 521
\par
functions from linguistic interpretations of a subject. It
\par
generates output values through compositional infer-
\par
ence rules and a defuzzification algorithm. Details
\par
about Mamdani-style fuzzy inference can be found in
\par
Mamdani (1974) and Negnevitsky (2005). A brief
\par
workflow showing how our fuzzy system derives the
\par
