System output surface
\par
To evaluate the performance of a Mamdani-style
\par
fuzzy system, we used its three-dimensional output
\par
surface following the suggestion by Negnevitsky
\par
(2005). A satisfactory system building is achieved
\par
through empirical tunings until the system generates a
\par
gradual changing surface which appropriately emu-
\par
lates subjective human reasoning regarding how the
\par
interactions of the system's inputs influence its output
\par
in the context the problem is viewed.
\par
The output surface of our system is shown in Fig. 4.
\par
The membership functions and the fuzzy rules men-
\par
tioned above are decided based by assessing this
\par
surface. The general trend should be that higher user
