\par
confidence levels and higher fitness of geographic
\par
context lead to higher trustworthiness, while certain
\par
special considerations should be appropriately reflected
\par
on the surface. For example, if a report has an extremely
\par
low user confidence level (meaning a very low user
\par
expertise), its trustworthiness should be very low even
\par
if its fitness of geographic context is high. Conversely,
\par
even if a report has an extremely low fitness of
\par
geographic context, its trustworthiness should be mod-
\par
erate if its user confidence level is very high.
\par
System usage with a running example
\par
Figure 5 shows an example of generating trustworthi-
\par
ness of a reporting (7.68) with two crisp inputs of
\par
confidence level (8) and fitness of geographic context
\par
(6.5). The red vertical line through the aggregate
\par
output fuzzy set depicts location of the COG.
\par
Once the system has generated the trustworthiness
\par
scores for a VGI dataset, a user-preferred threshold is
\par
used to reject or accept the reports. Non-outlier reports
\par
with trustworthiness scores lower than the threshold
\par
will be rejected and will be accepted if otherwise.
\par
Outlier reports should be specially treated. Outlier
\par
reports with trustworthiness scores lower than an
\par
assigned threshold can be simply discarded. However,
\par
outlier reports above the threshold should be treated
\par
with caution. It should be reserved or held for further
\par
observations, i.e., to see whether or not similar reports
\par
will be reported nearby to confirm it.
\par
The selection of threshold is context-dependent and
\par
subject to the accuracy requirements of specific
\par
projects. Setting a higher threshold can reduce the
\par
number of false positives (FP), but it will inevitably
\par
increase the number of false negatives (FN). Setting a
\par
lower threshold can reduce the number of FN, while it
\par
will increase the number of FP. In the context of VGI,
\par
FN is actually better than FP. Because rejecting good
\par
quality VGI incorrectly is actually better than accept-
\par
ing poor quality VGI incorrectly. Certainly one can
\par
choose a very high threshold to only collect VGI with
\par
extremely high trustworthiness scores, and ignoring
\par
FN.
\par
This system has been implemented using the
\par
following tools. DBSCAN algorithm was integrated
\par
to ArcGIS as an extension through Python. The fuzzy
\par
logic toolbox of MATLAB was used for performing
\par
the fuzzy inference. Figure 6 illustrates the architec-
\par
ture of the implemented system.
\par
Case study: a VGI-based crop pest surveillance
\par
Motivation
\par
VGI has been previously explored for location-based
\par
crop pest managements given its potential in fostering
\par
interactive digital communities in which farmers and
\par
experts collaboratively manage crop pest risks (Deng
\par
and Chang 2012; Suen et al. 2014). In a VGI-based
\par
pest management system, the task of acquiring
\par
Fig. 5 Fuzzy inference process for an assumed user report
\par
123526 GeoJournal (2017) 82:517--532
\par
Fig. 6 Architecture of the system implementation
\par
geospatial data of crop pest surveillances is delegated
\par
to farmers to share their location-based observations.
\par
Information relevant to managing crop pests is then
\par
discovered from the shared surveillance data through
\par
various spatiotemporal analytics and subsequently
\par
disseminated to the farmers for them to better manage
\par
crop pest risks.
\par
Inspired by these previous studies, in order to
\par
demonstrate the usefulness of the fuzzy system in
\par
handling VGI quality, the system was adopted to
\par
measure the quality of a set of crop pest surveillance
\par
reports collected in Xiajiang prefecture of Jiangxi
\par
province, China.
\par
Study design and data analysis
\par
VGI collection and quality assessment using the fuzzy
\par
system
\par
The major crop type cultivated in Xiajiang prefecture
\par
was rice which accounted for around 90 \% of the total
\par
cropland of the prefecture (216 km2). Two hundred
\par
local rice farmers distributed across the prefecture
\par
were recruited to conduct a rice pest surveillance.
\par
The pest surveillance was conducted by the farmers
\par
from 15 to 25 August, 2014. They reported rice pest
\par
incidents (pest occurrences, damages, or both)
\par
observed during their daily farming activities. To
\par
report an observed pest incident, the observer inserted
\par
a flat bamboo chip firmly into the soil of the rice paddy
\par
where the pest incident was observed. The observer
\par
also recorded the species name, observation time, and
\par
confidence level on the bamboo chip. No other
\par
coaching to the farmers was conducted to ensure a
\par
minimum intervention to the user contributions. After
\par
the pest surveillance, we collected the geographic
\par
coordinates of the inserted bamboo chips using
\par
Trimble\`O GeoXT handheld GPS devices which
\par
delivered a 50 cm positioning accuracy.
\par
Various rice pest incidents were reported, the
\par
species included mainly rice stem borers, rice leaf
\par
rollers, rice plant hoppers, rice water weevils, and
\par
mole cricket. Of the species, the rice stem borers'
\par
scope of activity was relatively fixed over time. It thus
\par
would be easier to conduct post-surveys to verify the
\par
actual presences of the reported rice stem borer
\par
incidents. Rice stem borer incident reports were
\par
therefore used to evaluate the usefulness of the
\par
system. During the pest surveillance period, 209 rice
\par
stem borer incident reports were collected.
\par
The quality of the 209 incident reports were
\par
assessed using the fuzzy system. A threshold should
\par
be assigned to the generated trustworthiness scores of
\par
the reports to determine whether or not a report should
\par
be accepted. As mentioned above, one can set a high
\par
threshold to only collect VGI with extremely high
\par
trustworthiness scores and ignore FN. In this case
\par
study, however, we intended to preserve as many
\par
reports as possible. Thus, a moderate threshold is more
\par
appropriate. We used a range of thresholds, from 4 to 6
\par
with an increment of 0.2, to evaluate the performance
\par
differences. Outliers, if any, were specially treated
\par
according to the method stated in the ``System usage
\par
with a running example'' section. The system gener-
\par
ated categorical results, i.e., accepted, rejected, and
\par
withheld.
\par
123GeoJournal (2017) 82:517--532 527
\par
Ground truth data collection
\par
From 26 August to 1 September, 2014 (immediately
\par
after the pest surveillance conducted by the farmers), a
\par
field pest survey was conducted by the pest manage-
\par
ment experts from the local agricultural department to
\par
verify the actual presences of the 209 reported rice
\par
stem borer incidents. The experts scrutinized the
\par
evidences including feeding wounds or holes, larval
\par
frass, egg masses, damage symptoms, and pupas of the
\par
stem borers within a two-meter buffer zone (consid-
\par
ering the mobility of the borers) surrounding each
\par
bamboo chip. If none of these evidences could be
\par
detected within the buffer zone of a report, the report
\par
was rejected by the experts; and was accepted if
\par
otherwise. The pest survey thus generated categorical
\par
results, i.e., reports being accepted and reports being
\par
rejected. Since the survey was conducted by experi-
\par
enced experts, the results of which were considered as
\par
accurate ground truth data.
\par
Conformity tests
\par
Subsequently, conformity tests were conducted. For
\par
each threshold within the interval [4, 6], a Cohen's
\par
kappa statistic (Viera and Garrett 2005) showing the
\par
degree of agreement between the fuzzy system-
\par
generated results and the pest survey results was
\par
calculated. The sensitivity (Eq. 8) and specificity
\par
(Eq. 9) were also calculated, respectively. Note that
\par
the reports in withheld status were not included in the
\par
calculations. The system-generated results corre-
\par
sponding to the highest kappa value were mapped
\par
for visualization, for which a confusion matrix was
\par
provided to show the details about the degree of
\par
agreement.
\par
Sensitivity{$\frac{1}{4}$} TP=\dh TP \th{} FN\TH; \dh 8\TH{}
\par
Specificity{$\frac{1}{4}$} TN=\dh TN \th{} FP\TH; \dh 9\TH{}
\par
where TP, TN, FP, and FN represent true positive, true
\par
negative, false positive, and false negative,
\par
respectively.
\par
To evaluate the impacts of sample sizes on the system
\par
performance, randomization tests were performed.
\par
Using the threshold that corresponded to the highest
\par
kappa value and following the method mentioned
\par
above, 30 rounds of conformity test using 30 groups
\par
of different subsets from the whole sample set were
\par
conducted. That is, ten groups of 90 \%-subset, ten
\par
groups of 60 \%-subset, and ten groups of 30 \%-subset
\par
were randomly extracted from the whole dataset to
\par
conduct the conformity tests. Cohen's kappa statistic
\par
was calculated for each run of the tests. Mean and
\par
standard deviation were calculated for the Cohen's
\par
kappa statistics from each of the same percentile subsets.
\par
Results
\par
The fuzzy system identified from the 209 rice stem
\par
borer incident reports eight clusters and 16 outliers
\par
(Fig. 7a) and trustworthiness scores from 0.51 to 9.10
\par
(Fig. 7b).
\par
The results of the conformity tests using different
\par
thresholds are shown in Table 1. The sensitivity and
\par
specificity values confirm that elevating and lowering
\par
the thresholds can increase the numbers of FN and FP,
\par
respectively, leading to lower kappa values. The highest
\par
kappa value (0.67) corresponds to the thresholds 4.8 and
\par
five. Therefore, for the purpose of this case study, the
\par
integer five was adopted as the threshold for further
\par
analyses, although the threshold 4.8 obtained a same
\par
kappa value as the threshold five did.
\par
With a threshold five, see Fig. 7c, the fuzzy system
\par
rejected 29 reports, including six outliers with trustwor-
\par
thiness scores lower than five (red circles), and accepted
\par
170 reports (green circles). Ten outlier reports were held
\par
(blue circles) due to their relatively high user confidence
\par
levels associated. The conformity test showed that
\par
around 91 \% of the fuzzy system-generated results
\par
agreed to the survey results with a corresponding kappa
\par
value 0.67. Details are visualized by the confusion
\par
matrix shown in Fig. 8. Regarding the ten pest incident
\par
reports that were held, eight of which had in fact
\par
suffered infestations according to the survey results.
\par
Finally, using the threshold five, it was observed that
\par
the system performed better with larger sample sizes, as
\par
the mean kappa values increased with the increase of
\par
sample size (Fig. 9). The standard deviations also
\par
decreased with the increase of sample size (Fig. 9).
\par
Discussion
\par
Features of the system
\par
Using the pest surveillance data, we demonstrated that
\par
a proper use of fuzzy set theory can lead to desired
