\par
VGI quality assurance results. The fuzzy system was
\par
developed based on the idea that the quality of VGI
\par
can be assured based on its geographic context and
\par
provenance of user expertise, and trust can be used as a
\par
proxy of quality. Fuzziness involved in trust
\par
123
\par
judgement requires special attentions, and quality
\par
itself is also inherently fuzzy. Therefore, fuzzy set
\par
theory was adopted as the key to the system design,
\par
which easily incorporates semantic knowledge into
\par
the quality assessment. Bordogna et al. (2014a)
\par
promote a linguistic decision making approach to
\par
assess the quality of VGI. Our system extends their
\par
work by demonstrating the utility of fuzzy set theory in
\par
assessing the quality of user-contributed species
\par
surveillance reports in particular.
\par
The system design echoes the view of van Exel
\par
et al. (2010) that assessing VGI quality must consider
\par
not only feature quality and user quality but also the
\par
interdependency between them. To account for fitnessGeoJournal (2017) 82:517--532 529
\par
Fig. 9 Means and standard deviations (shown as whiskers)
\par
calculated for the Cohen's kappa statistics from the three groups
\par
of percentile subsets extracted for testing the impacts of sample
\par
sizes
\par
of geographic context (feature quality), DBSCAN
\par
clustering was used for identifying VGI clusters. As
\par
pointed out by Goodchild and Li (2012), quality
\par
measures of VGI can arise from the data themselves.
\par
More credit can be given to a clustering of similar
\par
reports than to a single report, in which case one can
\par
develop metrics of quality based on the clustered
\par
reports. In our system, the metric is based on the
\par
proximities of user reports to their corresponding
\par
cluster centers, so as to measure the reports' fitness of
\par
geographic context. It resembles Gao et al. (2014) in
\par
which a distance-decay function is used to measure the
\par
memberships of a cluster of VGI points assigned to
\par
Harvard University campus. The closer a point was to
\par
the campus core area, the higher membership the point
\par
obtained. Similarly, Liu et al. (2010) used an interpo-
\par
lation procedure to measure the weights of candidate
\par
point locations assigned to South China region. The
\par
closer a location was to the core area of South China
\par
region, the higher weight the point obtained. In
\par
addition, confidence was used as a surrogate to
\par
represent provenance of user expertise (user quality).
\par
The case study confirmed that requesting the volun-
\par
teers to self-evaluate the correctness of their observa-
\par
tions was useful for assessing the quality of the
\par
generated information. By considering the interde-
\par
pendency between the feature quality and user quality,
\par
the fuzzy system could detect those VGI which
\par
seemed to fit geographic context well but virtually
\par
were of high uncertainty.
\par
Through the compositional inference rules (Fig. 3),
\par
the system can appropriately emulate human reason-
\par
ing about how the interactions of the two system input
\par
variables influence the system output (Fig. 4). Using a
\par
simple linear method, for example, combining the
\par
values of the two variables by summation does not
\par
have the same capability, which will be demonstrated
\par
by three exemplar input--output combinations in
\par
Table 2. For Combinations 1 and 2, the simple linear
\par
method obtains two identical values (i.e., 12), while
\par
the fuzzy system generates two different values (i.e.,
\par
3.6 and 5). This is an advantage of using rule-based
\par
fuzzy system. In Combination 1, although the fitness
\par
of geographic context is perfect, the fuzzy system
\par
treats the report as being less trustworthy than that of
\par
Combination 2 due to its overly low user confidence.
\par
In Combination 2, although the fitness of geographic
\par
context is relatively low, the system ranks the report
\par
with higher credibility as the user confidence is very
\par
high. In Combinations 1 and 3, using the simple linear
\par
method obtains two different values (i.e., 12 and 7.9),
\par
while the fuzzy system generates two identical values
\par
(i.e., 3.6). The former method gives more credit to
\par
Combination 1. However, for human judgement, it
\par
appears to be less appropriate due to its overly low user
\par
confidence level. Therefore, a fuzzy system with
\par
appropriately defined system parameters (e.g., mem-
\par
bership functions) can deal with such complicated
\par
non-linear cases through mimicking human thinking.
\par
Furthermore, VGI datasets are often large in
\par
volume as VGI contributors on the ground are
\par
ubiquitous. In the case study, although the entire
\par
dataset was not large, a trend was observed that the
\par
system's performance improved with increasing sam-
\par
ple size, rather than the opposite (Fig. 9). The system
\par
is also robust at handling non-clustered VGI (i.e.,
\par
outlier VGI detected by DBSCAN). Such VGI can be
\par
processed with two options, i.e., discarding (for
\par
reports with low user confidence levels) and holding
