\par
that outlier VGI should not be simply discarded.
\par
Finally, since user histories (provenance) can be
\par
harvested from VGI and fitness of geographic context
\par
can be determined from VGI, the approach also has a
\par
potential to be adapted and applied to similar VGI
\par
applications in different contexts, e.g., VGI-based
\par
earthquake casualty surveillances.
\par
Potential future improvement
\par
The fuzzy system should be extended in ways that
\par
generalize its applicability. Fuzzy logic enables tools
\par
to model the inherent fuzziness that would otherwise
\par
be neglected by traditional crisp logic, while it also
\par
introduces subjectivity into the modelling process.
\par
Imprecision related to subjectivity has often been cited
\par
as a limitation in conventional fuzzy systems (Ad-
\par
hikari and Li 2013; Al-kheder et al. 2008). In our
\par
study, although the selection and turnings of the
\par
system parameters are justified, they are still the
\par
results of a subjective process. Therefore, optimizing
\par
system parameters is perhaps the most important.
\par
Taking the case study for example, the highest
\par
kappa value obtained was 0.67 on a 91 \% agreement.
\par
Although 0.67 is considered a substantial agreement
\par
(Viera and Garrett 2005), the specificity (0.66) was not
\par
as good as the sensitivity (0.96) (Table 1). In order to
\par
preserve as many reports as possible while reducing
\par
false positive value, one solution is to further improve
\par
the system through parameter calibrations (e.g.,
\par
membership function calibrations) based on sensitiv-
\par
ity analyses using the pest survey data (ground truth
\par
data) collected in the case study. After the calibrations,
\par
the system can be generalized to larger spatiotemporal
\par
extents with greater reliability. Another solution is to
\par
use the consensus approach in which system param-
\par
eters are optimized based on the subjective opinions of
\par
multiple decision-makers (Zhang et al. 2014). How-
\par
ever, both solutions are often laborious and time-
\par
consuming. To remediate this problem, a machine
\par
learning approach, which involves the use of artificial
\par
neural network to determine the appropriate system
\par
parameters automatically, seems more promising as
\par
demonstrated in studies using the combined neuro-
\par
123
\par
fuzzy systems for understanding environmental qual-
\par
ity issues (e.g., Carnevale et al. 2009; Yan et al. 2010).
\par
It will be interesting to investigate how such systems
\par
can be utilized to better assure VGI quality.
\par
Moreover, in calculating fitness of geographic
\par
context, the spatial extent of a cluster is subject to
\par
the VGI points within the cluster. A small number of
\par
false contributions in a VGI cluster would not
\par
significantly affect the cluster's spatial extent (the
\par
spatial extent affects the memberships of the points
\par
within the cluster), especially not when point density
\par
of the clusters is high. However, if the majority of the
\par
contributions in a VGI cluster are false contributions,
\par
our approach will be less effective because the
\par
uncertainty about the spatial extent of the cluster is
\par
high. This problem points to the need to incorporate a
\par
user reputation database to our fuzzy system to
\par
exclude contributions from contributors with lower
\par
reputation before our system performs a refined data
\par
quality assurance work. The idea is similar to that in a
\par
facilitated-VGI system suggested in Cinnamon and
\par
Schuurman (2013).
\par
Conclusion
\par
In this paper a fuzzy system to assure the quality of VGI
\par
collected for the purposes of species surveillances is
\par
presented. With a growing number of volunteered
\par
geospatial data of species surveillances garnered from
\par
the general public, means for the VGI quality assurance
\par
are still limited. Developing robust computational
\par
approaches to assure the quality of VGI is crucial to
\par
the development of such public participatory surveil-
\par
lance programmes. The fuzzy system has the potential
\par
to benefit relevant experts, scientists, policy makers,
\par
and ordinary VGI users alike. Quantitatively, the
\par
usefulness of the system is demonstrated through a
\par
crop pest surveillance case study, although further
\par
calibrations of the system parameters will be needed.
\par
Qualitatively, the system has various features, including
\par
mainly its advantages in terms of linguistic fuzziness
\par
handling, geographic context measuring, provenance
\par
acquiring, and outlier treating. Nevertheless, future
\par
work is needed to establish a neuro-fuzzy system and a
\par
user reputation system to generalize its applicability.
