\par
A B S T R A C T
\par
Background: Many decision-analytic models with varying structures
\par
have been developed to inform resource allocation in chronic obstruc-
\par
tive pulmonary disease (COPD). Objectives: To review COPD models
\par
for their adherence to the best practice modeling recommendations
\par
and their assumptions regarding important aspects of the natural
\par
history of COPD. Methods: A systematic search of English articles
\par
reporting on the development or application of a decision-analytic
\par
model in COPD was performed in MEDLINE, Embase, and citations
\par
within reviewed articles. Studies were summarized and evaluated on
\par
the basis of their adherence to the Consolidated Health Economic
\par
Evaluation Reporting Standards. They were also evaluated for the
\par
underlying assumptions about disease progression, heterogeneity,
\par
comorbidity, and treatment effects. Results: Forty-nine models of
\par
COPD were included. Decision trees and Markov models were the
\par
most popular techniques (43 studies). Quality of reporting and
\par
adherence to the guidelines were generally high, especially in more
\par
recent publications. Disease progression was modeled through clinical
\par
staging in most studies. Although most studies (n{$\frac{1}{4}$} 43) had incorpo-
\par
rated some aspects of COPD heterogeneity, only 8 reported the results
\par
across subgroups. Only 2 evaluations explicitly considered the impact
\par
of comorbidities. Treatment effect had been mostly modeled (20) as
\par
both reduction in exacerbation rate and improvement in lung func-
\par
tion. Conclusions: Many COPD models have been developed, gener-
\par
ally with similar structural elements. COPD is highly heterogeneous,
\par
and comorbid conditions play an important role in its burden. These
\par
important aspects, however, have not been adequately addressed in
\par
most of the published models.
\par
Keywords: chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, cost-effectiveness,
\par
discrete-event simulation, Markov models, modeling.
\par
Copyright \& 2017, International Society for Pharmacoeconomics and
\par
Outcomes Research (ISPOR). Published by Elsevier Inc.
\par
Introduction
\par
Predicting the outcomes of alternative scenarios and policies is a
\par
central theme in many disciplines. Epidemiological projections,
\par
such as estimating the future prevalence of a given condition, can
\par
be used as a guide for policymakers for long-term planning.
\par
Predicting costs and health consequences associated with the
\par
adoption of competing health technologies informs technology
\par
adoption and resource allocation decisions. Challenges involved
\par
in such predictions include the availability of evidence from
\par
multiple sources, the need for long-term predictions beyond
\par
available data, and the requirement for translating evidence on
\par
intermediate outcomes to policy-relevant messages. Overcoming
\par
such challenges typically requires disease simulation and
\par
decision-analytic modeling. Given that such challenges prevail
\par
in almost all contexts in health care decision making, the use of
\par
decision-analytic modeling in medical decision making is con-
\par
sidered inevitable [1].
\par
The development and validation of a disease model is typi-
\par
cally a highly complex process requiring several fundamental
\par
assumptions related to, for example, natural history, impact of
\par
the health technologies on natural history, choice of model
\par
structure, relevant time horizon for the analysis, and the out-
\par
comes of interest [2].The next step is typically parameterization
\par
through evidence synthesis. Although it is unlikely to have a
\par
general consensus among the investigators along the process,
\par
exploring and understanding the different decisions that inves-
\par
tigators have made in the course of developing a disease model
\par
Con icts of interest: The authors declare no con icts of interest.
