\par
can inform and make more comprehensive the process of sub-
\par
sequent model development.
\par
The disease of focus for the present study is chronic obstruc-
\par
tive pulmonary disease (COPD), a progressive condition of the
\par
airways characterized by diminishing lung function and episodes
\par
of symptom worsening called exacerbations [3]. At present, COPD
\par
is estimated to be the fth cause of death worldwide, but studies
\par
project that it will become the third cause of death globally by
\par
2030 [4]. The signi cant economic and humanistic burden of
\par
COPD has caused many treatments and management strategies
\par
to emerge for prevention, diagnosis, and management of the
\par
disease [5,6]. Projection of the future burden of COPD and the
\par
requirement for economic evaluations of existing and emerging
\par
technologies have resulted in multiple COPD models. Under-
\par
standing the general characteristics of such models, such as the
\par
target population, model structure, and type of questions
\par
answered, can provide future investigators with a systematic
\par
and broad view of the COPD modeling landscape.
\par
In addition to the general features of the models, character-
\par
izing the COPD-speci c assumptions made in such models can
\par
support future model development and decision analysis in
\par
terms of comprehensiveness. COPD is a remarkably heteroge-
\par
neous disease [7], which suggests that the bene ts of interven-
\par
tions can differ among different subgroups of individuals. In
\par
addition, the devastating impact of comorbid conditions in COPD
\par
is well recognized [8]. One other potentially important assump-
\par
tion is how the effect of pharmacological treatments ismodeled,
\par
because clinical trials have evaluated the impact of pharmaco-
\par
therapy in terms of both change in the rate of lung function
\par
decline and change in the incidence of exacerbations. Finally,
\par
there are alternative choices for modeling COPD progression;
\par
some studies model disease progression directly through the
\par
continuum of lung function, whereas others model it indirectly
\par
by translating lung function decline to discrete clinical states
\par
de ned by the Global Initiative for Obstructive Lung Disease
\par
(GOLD) grades [3].
\par
The aim of this review was to synthesize the state of science
\par
in the eld by systematically exploring the characteristics of
\par
COPD models. Our review considered adherence to the best
\par
practice modeling guidelines as well as the assumptions made
\par
in COPD models relating to speci c aspects of the disease. We
\par
were interested in nding the areas of similarity as well as
\par
differences across published COPD models in search of oppor-
\par
tunities for potential improvement in decision-analytic modeling
\par
in the eld of COPD.
\par
duplicate, by the primary reviewer (Z.Z.) and then a secondary
\par
reviewer (T.C. or M.S.). Discrepancies between reviewers' ndings
\par
were discussed and resolved through consensus. Second, full-
\par
text analysis was performed by the primary reviewer, who
\par
identi ed the nal set of studies to be included in the analysis.
\par
A customized checklist was created to summarize key parame-
\par
ters of all simulation models. A second reviewer (A.K.) independ-
\par
ently extracted data from a random selection (10\%) of studies to
\par
ensure consistency in data extraction. Key information from the
\par
reviewed articles was extracted and categorized according to the
\par
following three groups: adherence to guidelines, model lineage
\par
(i.e., further development of a previously published model),
\par
and COPD assumptions.
\par
General Characteristics and Adherence to Reporting Guidelines
\par
We summarized the general characteristics of the models using a
\par
modi ed checklist that was initially based on a previously
\par
published study [10]. It included the target jurisdiction (country),
\par
authors, year of publication, type of model (models were classi-
\par
ed according to published taxonomies [11], summarized in
\par
Table 1), intervention, type of population (static population,
\par
which is the evolution of a xed cohort, and dynamic[or open]
\par
population, which incorporates arrival of new individuals during
\par
the study period), time horizon, cycle length (if applicable),
\par
perspective of the evaluation (e.g., third-party or societal), dis-
\par
count rate, how effect measure was modeled (if applicable), and
\par
whether indirect costs were included.
\par
We also evaluated the adherence of models to the health
\par
economic modeling report guidelines: the Consolidated Health
\par
Economic Evaluation Reporting Standards (CHEERS) [2].
\par
Model Lineage
\par
Often, disease models, once developed, are used for various
\par
purposes over time. This involves rounds of model expansion
\par
and revisions, generating various model versions based on the
\par
same model structure. Studying the evolution or lineage of such
\par
models over time provides another means for mapping the COPD
\par
modeling landscape. On the basis of statements made by authors
\par
and works cited in the articles, we mapped the lineage or history
\par
of the models.
\par
Methods
\par
A systematic review was undertaken on the basis of the Preferred
\par
Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses
\par
guidelines [9]. We performed a search in MEDLINE and Embase
\par
(completed on August 24, 2015) limited to articles in English. In
\par
addition, we performed manual searches on the reference lists of
\par
the included articles and consulted with experts for relevant
\par
publications. Details of the literature search are provided in
\par
Appendix I in Supplemental Materials found at http://dx.doi.
\par
org/10.1016/j.jval.2016.08.003.
\par
Studies that met the inclusion criteria were those that used a
\par
formal decision-analytic modeling approach to project the future
\par
burden of COPD or were studies that undertook a cost-
\par
effectiveness analysis of alternative interventions. Only studies
\par
with a primary focus on COPD were considered; therefore,
\par
publications considering COPD as an event or a complication of
\par
another condition were excluded.
\par
Study selection was carried out in two phases. First, title and
\par
abstract screening was performed, independently and in
\par
Exploration of COPD-Specific Assumptions
\par
We explored the COPD-speci c elements of each model with a
\par
focus on the areas of active research in COPD, which were de ned
\par
by the expert clinician of our team. The rst criterion considered
\par
was COPD progression. This could be modeled in terms of
\par
transition either through severity stages (e.g., GOLD grade) or
\par
through continuous changes in lung function metrics (e.g., forced
\par
expiratory volume in 1 second [FEV1]). Although GOLD grades are
\par
mainly based on FEV1 cutoffs, a xed transition rate across GOLD
\par
grades does not necessarily correspond to a xed rate of FEV1
\par
decline. The second criterion was the impact of pharmacotherapy
\par
in terms of assumptions about the impact of treatment on lung
\par
function or exacerbation rate. Given that exacerbation is a
\par
function of COPD severity (lung function), modeling treatment
\par
effect on lung function indirectly affects exacerbation rates. Many
\par
studies, however, have reported the direct impact of pharmaco-
\par
therapy on exacerbation rates, which might not necessarily be
\par
mediated through lung function. Furthermore, the assumptions
\par
regarding heterogeneity in the natural history of COPD were
\par
investigated. Studies were assessed in terms of whether they
\par
incorporated heterogeneity in model calculations and whether
\par
they reported results across subgroups. Finally, we determined
\par
whether COPD comorbidities were considered explicitly in
\par
the model.154
\par
