\par
Table 1-- Summary of different model structures that were included in the reviewed reports.
\par
Type of model Description
\par
MM DT DES ISM SD TSM MMs consist of a set of mutually exclusive states that patients can transition between at a cycle [11]. MMs are mostly
\par
used to project the trajectory of a cohort of individuals over time, through which between-individual variation (i.e.,
\par
heterogeneity) is usually forgone. MMs are very popular for disease modeling because of their simplicity of
\par
implementation. Nevertheless, two disadvantages attached to MMs are 1) the Markovian assumption underlying MMs
\par
that expresses that the future states depend only on present states and 2) MMs are not fully capable of re ecting an
\par
individual's trajectory over time (even if they are used to model individuals rather than a cohort, they give the
\par
probability of an individual being at different states at a cycle rather than a speci c state for that individual to be at
\par
that cycle).
\par
DT is a simulation technique in which an individual or a cohort of individuals can move to different states, or different
\par
events might occur with different probabilities [4]. DTs are intrinsically close to MMs with the difference that unlike
\par
MMs they are untimed [4]. Although the implementation of a DT model is straightforward, the fact that time is
\par
essentially ignored in a DT model is its biggest drawback.
\par
DES is a simulation technique in which the agent of a model is an individual rather than a cohort, with possible
\par
interaction between agents of the model [11]. DES is a capable framework for re ecting between-individual variation (i.
\par
e., heterogeneity) and modeling the trajectory of an individual over time [11]. Nevertheless, probabilistic
\par
implementation of such models requires a high computational capacity.
\par
ISM is similar to DES when the agent of a model is an individual; nevertheless, unlike DES, there is no interaction
\par
between the model's agents in ISM [11].
\par
SD is another simulation technique for modeling a cohort, through which differential equations inform the present
\par
states within the model [11]. SDs are not capable of modeling heterogeneity and their probabilistic implementation
\par
requires high computational capacity.
\par
TSM has a similar concept as an MM because there are some mutually exclusive states that at each cycle contain a
\par
proportion of the cohort [56]. The difference between TSM and MM is that there are no transition probabilities in TSM,
\par
which simply relaxes the underlying Markovian assumption of MMs.
\par
