\par
Results
\par
The search resulted in 4054 references, excluding duplicates.
\par
During title and abstract screening, 3911 citations were excluded,
\par
resulting in 143 articles for full-text analysis. The agreement
\par
between reviewers in the screening process was very good
\par
({$\kappa$} statistic 0.89) [12]. After full-text review,49 publications met
\par
the inclusion criteria (Fig. 1) [9].
\par
Summary of General Characteristics of Models and Their
\par
Adherence to CHEERS
\par
Table 2 presents a summary of the 49 models. A wide range of
\par
simulation modeling approaches had been applied: 41 studies
\par
were Markov models [13--53], 2 were decision trees [54,55], 1 used
\par
a time-in-state model [56], 2 used an individual sampling model-
\par
ing approach [57,58], 1 was a discrete-event simulation [59], and 2
\par
were system dynamics models[60,61].
\par
Most of the models (n{$\frac{1}{4}$} 40) were developed for the purpose of
\par
economic evaluation, either of alterative COPD treatments or of a
\par
COPD management program [13--22,24--28,30--34,37--39,42--52,54--
\par
56,58,60,61]. Two models [40,41] were developed to project the
\par
future burden of COPD; ve models [23,29,35,36,53] represented a
\par
case study for methodological work; and two models [57,59] were
\par
developed as a generic modeling framework (multipurpose and
\par
not an ad hoc model). Seven models [16,33,40,41,49,60,61] were
\par
open population (dynamic) models.
\par
Table 3 presents the results of the assessment of adherence to
\par
the CHEERS guideline [2]. Studies generally did well in following
\par
the reporting guideline (even though several articles did not
\par
explicitly state adherence to CHEERS or any other guideline).
\par
Nevertheless, the reporting of some items was commonly
\par
neglected, such as details of the analytical methods (only 34
\par
articles explicitly described the analytical methods), reporting of
\par
probabilistic distributions along with their parameters (only 25
\par
articles comprehensively mentioned deterministic and probabil-
\par
istic distributions of parameters), and the evaluation of hetero-
\par
geneity through subgroup-speci c results (only 8 articles reported
\par
the results per subgroup).
\par
Model Lineage
\par
Figure 2 depicts the lineage of all the published models. As can be
\par
seen, a Markov model by Oostenbrink et al. [24], which was
\par
developed for the Netherlands and Canada, was the most widely
\par
adopted model structure, used in eight subsequent studies (seven
\par
for European jurisdictions and one not explicitly mentioning its
\par
jurisdiction). Other widely adopted model structures were from
\par
Borg et al. [13], Price et al. [15], Spencer et al. [18], Hoogendoorn
\par
et al. [33], Sin et al. [37], Buist et al. [40], and Asukai et al. [57].
\par
These adopted models, along with their``offspring,'' are pre-
\par
sented in Figure 2.
\par
Review of Models with Regard to COPD-Specific Features
\par
In terms of modeling COPD progression, 41 studies [13--
\par
44,46,48,49,51--53,56,60,61] modeled transition across GOLD
\par
grades, whereas only 2 studies [57,58] modeled progression
\par
through FEV1 decline. FourMarkov models [45,47,54,55] used
\par
exacerbation status in de ning model states, and 1study modeled
\par
COPD through states de ned by the maintenance therapy usage
\par
[50]. One study [59] did not clearly mention how the authors
\par
modeled the disease progression.
\par
For the most part, treatment effect was modeled as a direct
\par
reduction in exacerbation rate without any impact on lung
\par
function [20,21,32,34--39,44--46,50,52,54,55]. Several other studies,
\par
however, modeled the impact of treatment in improving lung
\par
function either without [14,33,42,43,51,53,56] or with [13,15--
\par
19,21,23--26,28--31,48,49,57,58,60] a simultaneous impact on reduc-
\par
