\par
Most studies incorporated at least some aspects of disease
\par
heterogeneity into their main analysis through subgroup-level
\par
strati cation. The most popular subgroup variables were baseline
\par
disease severity, sex, and age. Nevertheless, only eight studies
\par
[22,23,32,33,41,42,51,60] clearly reported results of subgroup-
\par
speci c analyses.
\par
Only two models, those by Lock et al. [14] and Zaniolo et al.
\par
[46], explicitly incorporated the impact of comorbidities. The
\par
former evaluated the cost-effectiveness of smoking cessation,
\par
and the latter the cost-effectiveness of tiotropium bromide. In
\par
both studies, the authors acknowledged the importance of
\par
comorbid conditions in the context of their evaluation. Some
\par
other models indirectly considered the impact of comorbidity.
\par
Price et al. [58] mentioned comorbidity as a predictor for calcu-
\par
lating utility values. In the study by Bilge and Saka [59], comor-
\par
bidity was mentioned to be``a variable in the model;
\par
nevertheless, the exact nature and impact of such a variable
\par
were not discussed explicitly.
\par
Discussion
\par
We performed a systematic review of simulation models of COPD
\par
that have been used for burden of disease projections or
\par
economic evaluation of interventions and programs for COPD.
\par
Our systematic review identi ed 49 models. There are some
\par
general similarities among the studies. For example, most of
\par
the studies (n{$\frac{1}{4}$} 43) used a Markov modeling approach. Modeling
\par
the progression of COPD on the basis of transition through
\par
established clinical grading systems was a common feature.
\par
There was, however, greater heterogeneity in modeling the
\par
speci c aspects of COPD including assumptions of treatment
\par
effects and disease progression. Generally, models did well in
\par
complying with the standard modeling reporting guidelines.
\par
There were some limitations in our study. First, only English
\par
articles were included in our search. Second, model development
\par
is a very complex process containing many elements ranging
\par
from evidence synthesis to deciding on underlying assumptions
\par
to identify the relevant outcomes. Describing all such elements in
\par
detail in a single article is not feasible. As such, there has been a
\par
certain level of subjectivity in our assessment of published
\par
models. A more comprehensive assessment of models would
\par
involve access to the models' codes and requesting additional
\par
details from the authors. Running identical scenarios on different
\par
models as well as enabling or disabling some of the model
\par
features would provide critical insight into the impact of different
\par
assumptions on the variability in the reported results.
\par
Several models had included some aspects of heterogeneity in
\par
the natural history of COPD, mainly through strati cation of
\par
analysis on patient characteristics. Nevertheless, given the extent
\par
of heterogeneity in the natural history of COPD, and that only a
\par
fraction of such heterogeneity can be explained by observable
\par
traits [62,63], our conclusion is that heterogeneity was not fullyTable 2-- Summary of the simulation models in COPD.
