\par
accounted for. In cohort models (e.g., Markov models), violation
\par
of the homogeneity principle can cause bias in the estimated
\par
outcomes, even when the outcome of interest is cost-
\par
effectiveness for the whole population [64]. Cohort models should
\par
be strati ed on subgroups such that each subgroup can be
\par
considered a homogeneous population. If the creation of many
\par
subgroups is required to account for heterogeneity, then cohort
\par
models can become unwieldy. In such instances, the use of
\par
microsimulation (individual-level modeling) is recommended
\par
[11]. In addition to this technical requirement, we think there
\par
are other reasons to encourage the use of microsimulations. As
\par
the emphasis on individualizing care (personalized medicine)
\par
gains momentum, so should the analytical platforms for evalua-
\par
tion of individualized care. Individual-level modeling has the
\par
capacity to provide results that can guide decision making for
\par
individual patients on the basis of his or her clinical traits.
\par
Individual-level models provide an ideal framework for modeling
\par
heterogeneity that is not accounted for by observable traits (e.g.,
\par
through assigning random-effect terms to simulated individuals).
\par
It is, however, important to note that implementing such
\par
individual-level modeling would, generally, come with more
\par
complexity and a longer development time.
\par
Another important aspect of the burden of COPD that was
\par
largely ignored in the published models is comorbidity [8]. For
\par
many interventions, such as smoking cessation, the need for the
\par
incorporation of other diseases that are affected by smoking is
\par
clear. Nevertheless, even for interventions such as pharmaco-
\par
therapy for COPD, the impact of considering comorbid conditions
\par
is likely to be substantial. Evidence suggests that the degree of
\par
lung function impairment is correlated with the incidence of
\par
comorbid conditions, and there is an intricate bidirectional causal
\par
relationship between comorbid conditions such as heart failure
\par
and COPD exacerbations [65]. As such, impact of an intervention
\par
targeted at COPD can be multifaceted. This is an area of future
\par
research in terms of both a better understanding of the causal
\par
relationships between COPD and comorbid conditions and the
\par
impact of such considerations in COPD models in terms of their
\par
results.
\par
Conclusions
\par
Many COPD models have been developed, generally with similar
\par
structural elements. Nevertheless, modeling practice in COPD is
\par
in need of improvement to more fully acknowledge the highly
\par
heterogeneous nature of the disease and the fact that comorbid
\par
conditions play an important role in its burden. These criticalV A L U E I N H E A L T H 2 0 ( 2 0 1 7 ) 1 5 2-- 1 6 2 161
\par
aspects have not been adequately addressed in most of the
\par
published COPD models.
