@article{zegaouiIdentificationAssessmentFunding2017,
  title = {Identification of Assessment and Funding Models Applicable to Companion Diagnostics in {{UK}}, France and {{Germany}}},
  author = {Zegaoui, Y. and Pacheco, L. and Teale, C. W.},
  year = 2017,
  journal = {Value in Health},
  volume = {20},
  number = {9},
  pages = {A702},
  publisher = {Elsevier},
  doi = {10.1016/j.jval.2017.08.1828},
  urldate = {2023-12-06},
  langid = {english},
  note = {A 7 0 2 VA L U E I N H E A LT H 2 0 ( 2 0 1 7 ) A 3 9 9 -- A 8 1 1
\par
Objectives: Along with the governmental pharmaceutical reform in Korea (2007),
\par
conditionally temporary reimbursement was applied to undergo Coverage Evidence
\par
Development (CED) in 2011 for 88 drugs which pose uncertainty. Recently the last
\par
appraisal has finished as of June, 2017. This study aims to look back the journey
\par
of CED in the course of the drug reevaluation in Korea. Methods: Through policy
\par
documents, HIRA's public and internal data and press release information, we out-
\par
lined the whole process of Coverage Evidence Development in the drug reevalu-
\par
ation -- introduction, implementation, evaluation and decision making - and
\par
described the outcome, lessons and the challenges in practice. Results: During
\par
the reevaluation, the delisting or the price-cut was extensively done. Having in
\par
difficulty where binomial decision, the Managed Entry was considered and the
\par
Committee suggested CED for the 10 active gradients 156 drugs (2011) which were
\par
gastrointestinal drugs. Prospective phase III non-inferiority RCTs were designed
\par
and the protocols were approved (2011). Of trials for 8 substances, 7 substances
\par
were terminated and released peer reviewed publication (2012\textasciitilde 2013). HIRA and the
\par
Committee reevaluated whether reimbursable (2014) for 7 substances 88 drugs, 87
\par
drugs came to be remained to reimbursable (the last one ongoing). In case of no
\par
termination, it was delisted and payback. Although CED was well accepted in the
\par
beginning, some clinical trial was discontinued or went a controversial lawsuit in
\par
the late. cOnclusions: As a result of CED, six substances have produced clinical
\par
evidence resolving/reducing uncertainty. It was valuable but resource-consuming
\par
decision so that it can be improved by real world evidence and registry use. Recently
\par
in Korea, off-label use for cancer drugs (2011), Conditional Treatment Risk Sharing
\par
scheme (2013), coverage for use within clinical research (2016) has been introduced.
\par
Lessons from this CED case will be helpful for those quasi-situations.
\par
PHP287
\par
Advanced tHerapy Medicinal Products -- Transformational Patient
\par
Benefits But Destined for Commercial Failure?
\par
Macaulay R
\par
PAREXEL International, London, UK
\par
Objectives: Advanced therapy medicinal products (ATMPs) encompass gene
\par
therapies, cell therapies, and tissue-engineered products. The first ATMP
\par
(ChondroCelect\textregistered ) was approved by the European Commission (EC) in 2009. These
\par
therapies offer potentially transformational/curative patient benefits in severe dis-
\par
eases of very high unmet need. In April 2017, Glybera\textregistered, the first EC-approved gene
\par
therapy (in 2012) and most expensive drug ({$>$} \texteuro{} 1 million/patient), announced they
\par
would not seek marketing authorisation renewal once it expires in November 2017
\par
for commercial reasons, emphasising the significant reimbursement challenges
\par
that such therapies may face. This research investigates the commercial success of
\par
all ATMPs marketed to date. Methods: EC-approved ATMPs were identified from
\par
the EMA website and a targeted review of relevant company press releases were
\par
undertaken to extract commercial information (up to 5th May 2017) Resul ts: Eight
\par
ATMPs have received EC marketing authorisation. Half (ChondroCelect\textregistered, Glybera\textregistered,
\par
MACI\textregistered, Provenge\textregistered ) have been withdrawn by their manufacturers, all for commercial
\par
reasons after an average of 3.5 years on the market (range: 1-7 years). These also rep-
\par
resent the first four approved ATMPs. Four others (Holoclar\textregistered, Imlygic\textregistered, Strimvelis\textregistered,
\par
Zalmoxis\textregistered ) have been EC-approved since 2015/6 with very limited information on
\par
their commercial success to date. GSK did announce that Strimvelis\textregistered{} was reim-
\par
bursed for its first patient in March 2017, despite being EC-approved since May
\par
2016 under a full ``money-back guarantee'' performance-based reimbursement
\par
scheme. cOnclusions: The first four EC-approved ATMPs have all been with-
\par
drawn from market for commercial reasons. This emphasizes the magnitude of the
\par
commercial challenges faced by ATMPs including complex and costly regulatory
\par
and manufacturing processes, small patient populations with limited clinical data,
\par
and the need for high upfront investment for a course of therapy that may only
\par
be a single treatment. Innovative payment models such as annuities/leasing and/
\par
or performance-based reimbursement may need to be explored to ensure patient
\par
access and commercial returns.
\par
PHP288
\par
DeveloPIng a Multi-Criteria Framework for Innovation and
\par
bIologic Drugs Assessment Decision Making in Egypt
\par
Fouad S1, ElMordy B2
\par
1Pharmacoeconomic Unit, Central Administration for Pharmaceutical Affairs, Ministry of Health,
\par
Egypt, Cairo, Egypt, 2Pricing department, Central Administration for Pharmaceutical Affairs,
\par
Ministry of Health, Egypt, Cairo, Egypt
\par
Objectives: Given the unmet medical needs, the search is optimal for achiev-
\par
ing the balance between satisfaction of patient need, offering the fair price to the
\par
company and the best utilization of health care resources. This is even tougher
\par
for Egypt, Our analysis demonstrates that firstly, currently adopted reference and
\par
ceiling ration criteria was the key driver of the economic evaluation and pricing
\par
decisions and no other factors were considered. Second, Many of Innovative and
\par
biologic drugs according to current ICER are not cost-effective, due to high cost per
\par
QALY or when the benefits not captured by the QALY. Third, there is absence of the
\par
sufficient data about how the new drug will be used in practice. We aim to develop
\par
a local MCDA framework for evaluation and selection of innovation and biologic
\par
drugs. Provide practice guidelines for its application in pricing and reimbursement
\par
decisions. Methods: Mapping out the proposed MCDA framework were based on
\par
a mixed approach: literature review, local document analysis, interviews with stake-
\par
holders. Firstly a systematic literature review was conducted to elicit the criteria
\par
to be used in the decision making process. Subsequently, the `importance' of the
\par
core set of criteria was elicited with different stakeholder including policy makers,
\par
payers, professionals, academia, representatives of patients and pharma industry.
\par
Finally, validation the MCDA list. Results: Based on our findings we suggest that
\par
the main criteria that can be the core of MCDA models for the assessment in our
\par
local setting are Health benefits and outcomes, Disease description, innovation level,
\par
the evidence provided and the socio-economic impact. cOnclusions: MCDA is
\par
believed to be helpful tool to integrate different aspects into health-care decision.
\par
Also to demonstrate true drug benefit and enhance patient access where there is
\par
huge unmet need: by 1- Adapting different P\&R outcomes compared to the standard
\par
process now. 2- Including wider societal perspectives.
\par
PHP289
\par
Implementing Managed Entry Agreements in Practice: tHe Dutch
\par
Reality cHeck
\par
Makady A1, van Veelen A2, de Boer A2, Hillege JL3, Klungel O2, Goettsch W1
\par
1The National Healthcare Institute (ZIN), Diemen, The Netherlands, 2Utrecht University, Utrecht,
\par
The Netherlands, 3University of Groningen, University Medical Center Groningen, Groningen,
\par
The Netherlands
\par
Objectives: In 2006, the National Healthcare Institute (ZIN) initiated conditional
\par
financing (CF) of expensive hospital drugs as an example of conditional reimburse-
\par
ment schemes (CRS). CF is a 4-year procedure encompassing initial HTA assess-
\par
ment (T=
\par
0) followed by additional data collection via outcomes research (separately
\par
assessing appropriate use \& cost effectiveness in routine practice) and re-assess-
\par
ment (T=
\par
4). This study aims to retrospectively evaluate the CF framework, focusing
\par
on Health Technology Assessment (HTA) procedures. Methods: All dossiers for
\par
drugs that underwent the full CF procedure were reviewed. Using a standardised
\par
data extraction form, 2 researchers independently extracted information on pro-
\par
cedural, methodological and decision-making aspects from HTA reports of drugs
\par
selected for CF. Results: 49 drugs were nominated for CF, of which 44 underwent
\par
T=
\par
0 assessments and 12 underwent the full procedure including T=
\par
4 assessments.
\par
The procedure extended beyond 4 years for 11/12 drugs. Particular components of
\par
HTA reports (e.g. value of information analyses) were missing from retrieved docu-
\par
ments, contrary to guidelines. Outcomes research studies conducted between T=
\par
0
\par
and T=
\par
4 provided insufficient scientific data for 5/12 drugs. After re-assessment,
\par
continuation of reimbursement was advised for 10/12 drugs, with 6 drugs necessitat-
\par
ing yet additional conditions for evidence generation. Notably, advice to discontinue
\par
reimbursement for 2/12 drugs has not yet been implemented in Dutch healthcare
\par
practice. cOnclusions: Theoretically, CF provides a valuable option for enabling
\par
quick but conditional access to medicines in the Netherlands. However, procedural,
\par
methodological and decision-making considerations related to scheme design and
\par
implementation may affect its value in decision-making practice. Shortcomings and
\par
learnings identified should inform recommendations for the development of future
\par
managed entry agreements worldwide.
\par
PHP290
\par
Identification of Assessment and Funding Models aPPlicable to
\par
